        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-figure-4-stretch", "Banded Figure-4 Stretch", "Glutes", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded figure-4 stretch loops a band around one thigh and pulls the crossed ankle toward the chest while lying down, deepening the glute stretch. Keep the head and shoulders relaxed on the floor and let the band set the depth."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-fire-hydrant", "Banded Fire Hydrant", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The banded fire hydrant lifts the knee sideways from a quadruped position against a band above the knees, targeting the glute medius. Keep the hips square to the floor and the torso quiet; small, controlled arcs beat big swings."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-glute-bridge", "Banded Glute Bridge", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded glute bridge bridges the hips up with a band pulling the knees inward, so the glutes must push out as well as extend. Keep the band under constant tension at the top and squeeze hard; the knees should track over the toes, never cave."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-good-morning", "Banded Good Morning", "Hamstrings", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The band good morning is an exercise targeting the hamstrings and glutes. It is often performed in powerlifting-style training for high reps, or in the place of barbell good mornings."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-hamstring-stretch", "Banded Hamstring Stretch", "Hamstrings", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded hamstring stretch lies on the back with a band looped around the foot and draws the straight leg toward the chest. Keep the other leg heavy on the floor and the knee of the working leg long; the band sets the depth, not the arm strength."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-hip-thrust", "Banded Hip Thrust", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded hip thrust drives the hips to extension against a band looped over the pelvis and anchored under the feet. The resistance rises exactly where the glutes are strongest, at lockout; tuck the pelvis and finish each rep with a hard squeeze."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-it-band-stretch", "Banded IT-Band Stretch", "Hip Abductors", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded IT-band stretch lies on the back with a band around the foot and draws the straight leg across the body toward the opposite shoulder. Keep the pelvis and the opposite shoulder heavy on the floor; the stretch should run down the outer thigh, not wrench the knee."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-lat-stretch", "Banded Lat Stretch", "Lats", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded lat stretch holds a band overhead and leans sideways, letting the band lengthen the lats along the ribs. Anchor the hips and keep both feet grounded; breathe into the stretched side and let the arm travel with the band rather than fighting it."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-lateral-walk", "Banded Lateral Walk", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded lateral walk side-steps against a band around the ankles or knees, keeping constant outward tension on the glute medius. Stay low in an athletic quarter squat and never let the knees cave between steps; equal distances each way."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-romanian-deadlift", "Banded Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded Romanian deadlift hinges under a band anchored at the feet, where resistance increases through the lockout exactly as the glutes and hamstrings finish. Keep the spine long and push the hips back; the band makes the top of the rep the hardest part."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-shoulder-stretch", "Banded Shoulder Stretch", "Front Deltoids", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded shoulder stretch draws a band overhead and behind the back in a slow arc, opening the shoulders through their full range. Keep the elbows long and the grip adjustable, since narrower is harder; never force past a pinching shoulder."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-squat", "Banded Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded squat squats against a band anchored under the feet and over the shoulders, so the load grows toward lockout where a barbell is easiest. Keep the chest tall and the knees tracking out; the band rewards full, smooth extensions."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-sumo-walk", "Banded Sumo Walk", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded sumo walk walks forward and backward in a wide, low stance against a band around the knees or ankles, keeping constant outward tension on the hips. Stay in the quarter squat the whole time; short, wide steps and level hips."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-triceps-stretch", "Banded Triceps Stretch", "Triceps", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded triceps stretch holds a band overhead in one hand and draws the elbow back with the other, lengthening the triceps behind the arm. Keep the ribs down and the neck long; a gentle sustained pull with slow breathing loosens the elbow's lockout range."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-ab-rollout", "Barbell Ab Rollout", "Abs", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell roll-out is an abdominal exercise that utilizes a barbell in the place of an ab roller. It is best performed with a barbell that has rotating collars, and is considered more difficult than other ab roller variations. Many lifters may not be able to perform a single rep at first, but once they can perform these for reps, they'll be rewarded with a seriously strong core."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("squat", "Barbell Back Squat", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell back squat is a popular compound movement that emphasizes building the lower-body muscle groups and overall strength. It's the classic way to start a leg day, and is a worthy centerpiece to a lower-body training program. The squat is a competitive lift in the sport of powerlifting, but is also a classic measurement of lower-body strength. With the barbell racked on the traps or upper back, the emphasis is placed on the posterior chain but the entire body gets worked. The back squat can be trained in everything from heavy singles to sets of 20 reps or higher."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-press", "Barbell Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The bench press is a compound exercise that builds strength and muscle in the chest and triceps. When many people think of listing, the bench press is often the first exercise that comes to mind"),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-calf-raise", "Barbell Calf Raise", "Calves", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell calf raise stands with a bar on the back and drives the heels up through a full ankle extension. Keep the knees straight to bias the gastrocnemius, pause at the top and lower slowly through the full stretch; balance near a rack for safety."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-curl", "Barbell Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell curl is an arm exercise that is also one of the most recognizable movements in all of bodybuilding and fitness. It helps build sleeve-popping biceps and allows heavier loading than many other curl variations. It is usually performed in moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set, as part of the arm-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("deadlift", "Barbell Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell deadlift is a compound exercise used to develop overall strength and size in the posterior chain. It is a competition lift in the sport of powerlifting, but is also considered a classic benchmark of overall strength. When performed with the hands outside the knees, it is often called a \"conventional\" deadlift. When the feet are wide and the hands are inside the knees, it is a sumo deadlift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-front-raise", "Barbell Front Raise", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell front raise is an upper-body isolation exercise that targets the shoulders. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or higher, as part of the shoulder-focused part of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-glute-bridge", "Barbell Glute Bridge", "Glutes", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell glute bridge is a popular exercise targeting the muscles of the glutes and hamstrings. It can be done as a strength movement on its own, as an activation drill or warm-up for lower-body training, or as a burnout at the end of a lower-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hip-thrust", "Barbell Hip Thrust", "Glutes", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell hip thrust is an exercise that engages the posterior chain, particular the glutes. It involves fully extending your hips while holding a weighted barbell across the midsection."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-lunge", "Barbell Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell forward lunge is a popular lower-body exercise targeting the quads, glutes, and hamstrings. Using a barbell allows you to overload the exercise beyond body weight and perform the movement in strength or muscle-focused rep ranges. The forward lunge can be performed as part of a barbell complex, in a circuit, or on its own in the lower-body portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-overhead-extension", "Barbell Overhead Extension", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell overhead extension lowers a bar behind the head from a locked-out overhead grip and extends it back up, working the long head of the triceps through a deep stretch. Keep the elbows pointing forward and narrow, and the ribs down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ohp", "Barbell Overhead Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell overhead press presses a barbell from the shoulders to lockout with the legs quiet, the purest measure of upper-body pressing strength. Squeeze the glutes to lock the torso, move the head back out of the bar's path and finish with the bar over the mid-foot."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-preacher-curl", "Barbell Preacher Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The reverse-grip barbell curl is a variation on the biceps curl where the palms face downward. The switch from an underhand to an overhand grip brings the forearm and brachialis muscles more into the exercise. If it is uncomfortable to the wrists, try performing it on the angles of an EZ-curl bar."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-pullover", "Barbell Pullover", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell pullover arcs a bar from over the chest to behind the head and back while lying on a bench, stretching the lats and chest under load. Keep the elbows slightly bent and the hips steady; the movement happens at the shoulders, not the spine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-reverse-lunge", "Barbell Reverse Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell reverse lunge is a popular lower-body exercise targeting the quads, glutes, and hamstrings. Using a barbell allows you to overload the exercise beyond body weight and perform the movement in strength or muscle-focused rep ranges. The reverse lunge can be performed as part of a barbell complex, in a circuit, or on its own in the lower-body portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("shrug", "Barbell Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell shrug is an exercise targeting the traps (trapezius muscles). It is popular in strength and muscle-focused upper-body training, and is often trained on a shoulder day. With the assistance of straps, it can be loaded heavily, but it is still usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-10 reps per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("upright-row", "Barbell Upright Row", "Lateral Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell upright row is a barbell exercise that builds stronger and bigger traps. Many lifters combine this move with either their back or shoulder workout since it involves both body parts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-wrist-curl", "Barbell Wrist Curl", "Forearms", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell wrist curl rests the forearms on a bench or the thighs and hinges the wrists to curl the bar up, training the forearm flexors. Use a modest weight and a full range, from fully extended fingers to full flexion; slow negatives build the grip fastest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("battle-rope-double-slam", "Battle Rope Double Slam", "Front Deltoids", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The battle rope double slam drives both ropes overhead and slams them to the floor in one explosive motion, training the shoulders, core and hips as a single chain. It is usually performed for time or high reps, making it a staple of conditioning circuits and metabolic finishers."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("battle-ropes", "Battle Ropes", "Front Deltoids", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "Battle ropes are an explosive training tool that involves moving the rope in waves with the other end of the rope anchored at ground level. There are many different types of waves, but the most common is alternating waves with the left and right hands. The battle ropes can be performed for short bursts as power training, for longer durations as cardiovascular training, or as a component of a conditioning or fat-loss circuit."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bear-crawl", "Bear Crawl", "Front Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bear crawl is a popular bodyweight crawling exercise. While it looks simple, the bear crawl is an intense full-body movement that targets the cardiovascular system as well as a wide range of muscle groups, the shoulders, core, and legs in particular. It can be done as a full-body warm-up, in short bursts for power or circuit training, or for longer durations as cardiovascular conditioning."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("behind-the-back-barbell-shrug", "Behind the Back Barbell Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The barbell behind-the-back shrug is an exercise targeting the traps. Having the bar behind you helps keep your shoulders pulled up and back, rather than rounded forward. It is popular in strength and muscle-focused upper-body training, and is often trained on a shoulder day. With the assistance of straps, it can be loaded heavily, but it is still usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-10 reps per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("behind-the-neck-press", "Behind the Neck Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The behind-the-neck press presses a barbell from behind the head to lockout, demanding exceptional shoulder mobility. It builds the overhead position powerfully but is unforgiving of tight shoulders; only press from behind the neck if the bar can travel there without the head jutting forward."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("behind-the-neck-lat-pulldown", "Behind-the-Neck Lat Pulldown", "Lats", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The behind-the-neck pull-down is a cable exercise intended to target the muscles of the upper and middle back. If you have the requisite shoulder mobility, it can help you target the upper back muscles, including the trapezius and rhomboids, as well as the lats (latissimus dorsi). It is popular among elite bodybuilders and physique athletes, but some coaches consider it unsafe to the shoulder joint, particularly for people with existing shoulder problems."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("behind-the-neck-pull-ups", "Behind-the-Neck Pull-Up", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The behind-the-neck pull up pulls the bar down behind the head, an old-school variation that demands mobile shoulders and precise control. Only pull to a point behind the head without craning the neck forward; if the shoulders pinch or the head juts, stay with front pull ups."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-adductor-stretch", "Bench Adductor Stretch", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench adductor stretch opens the inner thighs by placing one leg on a bench and folding the torso toward it. Keep the standing knee soft and hinge from the hips rather than rounding the back, easing into the stretch until the adductors release."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-ankle-stretch", "Bench Ankle Stretch", "Calves", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench ankle stretch improves dorsiflexion by placing the ball of one foot on a bench and driving the knee forward over the toes. Keep the heel down and the movement slow, using short pulsing leans to loosen a stiff ankle before squatting or lunging."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-bulgarian-split-stretch", "Bench Bulgarian Split Stretch", "Abs", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The bench Bulgarian split stretch lengthens the hip flexors of the rear leg by draping that foot on a bench behind you and sinking the hips down and forward. Squeeze the rear glute to deepen the stretch safely; it doubles as the setup position for Bulgarian split squats."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-calf-stretch", "Bench Calf Stretch", "Calves", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench calf stretch places the forefoot on a bench with the heel anchored to the floor, then leans the torso in to lengthen the calf. Straighten the knee to bias the gastrocnemius or bend it slightly to reach the soleus."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-chest-stretch", "Bench Chest Stretch", "Pectorals", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench chest stretch lies face-up across a bench with the arms opened wide, letting gravity pull the shoulders toward the floor. It is a gentle way to open a tight chest after pressing work; bend the elbows more to shift the emphasis toward the shoulders."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-childs-pose", "Bench Child's Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The bench child's pose kneels in front of a bench and folds the arms onto it, lengthening the lats and spine without loading the knees. Breathe slowly into the sides of the ribcage and let the hips sink back with each exhale."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-couch-stretch", "Bench Couch Stretch", "Abs", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The bench couch stretch is a quad and hip-flexor opener that sets the rear foot up on a bench behind the body, then raises the torso upright. Tuck the pelvis and squeeze the rear glute before leaning back; it is one of the most effective stretches for desk-tightened hips."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-dips", "Bench Dips", "Triceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bench dip is a highly effective exercise for building the triceps. The resistance comes from the body itself, rather than weight."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-figure-4-glute-stretch", "Bench Figure-4 Glute Stretch", "Glutes", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench figure-4 glute stretch crosses one ankle over the opposite knee in a seated position and folds forward, stretching the glutes and hip rotators. Keep the back long rather than rounded and pull the crossed knee gently toward the chest to intensify it."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-hamstring-stretch", "Bench Hamstring Stretch", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench hamstring stretch rests one straight leg on a bench and hinges the chest toward the thigh. Keep both hips square and the knee of the elevated leg unlocked to target the hamstring without straining its attachments."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-lat-stretch", "Bench Lat Stretch", "Lats", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The bench lat stretch grips a bench with both hands and sinks the hips back and down, letting the torso fall between the arms. Push the chest toward the floor and rotate the palms slightly outward to widen the stretch through the lats."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-leg-pull-in", "Bench Leg Pull-In", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The bench leg pull-in sits on a bench and pulls the knees in toward the chest and back out, training the abs through a long, controlled range. Grip the bench for stability and keep the lower back from rounding at the extension; slow beats fast."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-bench-press", "Bench Press (Smith Machine)", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine bench press is a machine-based movement targeting the chest. The Smith machine mimics a barbell but provides more stability. This movement can be programmed in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle and strength endurance. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a chest or upper-body focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bench-pull", "Bench Pull", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The bench pull lies chest-down on a high bench and rows a barbell from beneath it, removing all body English from the pull. The chest stays planted so the lats and mid-back do the work; pull the bar to the underside of the bench and lower with control."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bent-arm-barbell-pullover", "Bent Arm Barbell Pullover", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The bent-arm barbell pull-over was a staple exercise of the \"Golden Era\" of bodybuilding and a favorite of Arnold Schwarzenegger himself. Done properly, the exercise targets the pecs, lats, and serratus anterior muscles. Old-school bodybuilders would perform them with light weights for sets of 20 between sets of heavy 20-rep squats, but pull-overs are more popular today in moderate rep ranges, such as 8-12 reps per set, as part of an upper-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-bent-over-row", "Bent Over Row (Smith Machine)", "Lats", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine bent-over row is an upper-body exercise targeting the muscles of the back, particularly the lats (latissimus dorsi). This movement can be programmed in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle and strength endurance. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of an upper-body or back-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bent-arm-ez-bar-pullover", "Bent-Arm EZ-Bar Pullover", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The bent-arm EZ-bar pullover lies on a bench and arcs the bar from over the chest to behind the head with the elbows bent, easing the demand on the shoulders while still stretching the lats and chest. Keep the elbows pointing up and the ribs down throughout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-row", "Bent-Over Barbell Row", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell bent-over row is a compound exercise used to build strength and size in both the lower and upper back. It targets nearly all of the muscles in the back, but particularly the lats, rhomboids, and lower back. It is commonly utilized as an accessory movement for the deadlift in powerlifting, but is also popular in strength and muscle-focused upper-body workouts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bent-over-db-row", "Bent-Over Dumbbell Row", "Lats", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell bent-over row is a popular exercise to develop and strengthen the muscles of the upper and middle back, like the latissimus (lat), lower trapezius, and rear deltoids. It can work equally well in strength, muscle-building, or circuit-style fat-loss workouts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bent-over-ez-bar-row", "Bent-Over EZ-Bar Row", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The bent-over EZ-bar row hinges forward and rows the angled bar to the lower ribs, the EZ grip turning the wrists into a more natural position for many lifters. Keep the torso close to parallel and drive the elbows past the ribs."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bicycle-crunch", "Bicycle Crunch", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bicycle crunch alternates bringing the opposite elbow toward the knee while the other leg extends, rotating through the trunk continuously. Keep the lower back light on the floor and rotate from the ribs rather than swinging the elbows; moderate speed, full rotation."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wrist-curl", "Bilateral Dumbbell Wrist Curl", "Forearms", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The bilateral dumbbell wrist curl curls both wrists together from a braced position, training the forearm flexors symmetrically. Keep the range full, from fingers hanging open to full flexion; light bells and high reps suit the wrists best."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bird-dog-hold", "Bird Dog Hold", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The bird dog is a core exercise designed to help strengthen and stabilize the posterior chain. It involves lifting an opposite-side arm and leg while in a bent-leg tabletop position. It is most commonly performed as part of a dynamic warm-up, or for mobility and muscle activation anytime."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bird-dog", "Bird-Dog", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bird dog is a core exercise designed to help strengthen and stabilize the posterior chain. It involves lifting an opposite-side arm and leg while in a bent-leg tabletop position. It is most commonly performed as part of a dynamic warm-up, or for mobility and muscle activation anytime."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("boat-pose", "Boat Pose", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Boat pose is a yoga hold that balances on the sitting bones with the legs and torso forming a V. It builds isometric strength through the deep abdominals and hip flexors; keep the chest lifted and the spine long rather than rounding to bring the legs higher."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bodyweight-calf-raise", "Bodyweight Calf Raise", "Calves", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The bodyweight calf raise drives the heels up from the floor through a full ankle extension, training the calves with nothing but bodyweight. Pause at the top and lower through the full stretch; hold a wall for balance but not for assistance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bodyweight-good-morning", "Bodyweight Good Morning", "Hamstrings", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bodyweight good morning hinges at the hips with the hands behind the head or at the chest, loading the hamstrings and lower back through a slow, deliberate bow. Keep the spine long and the knees soft; it is a warm-up and hinge-pattern drill rather than a strength lift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bodyweight-lateral-raise", "Bodyweight Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The bodyweight lateral raise raises the arms out to the sides with no added load, cueing the side delts in warm-ups and mobility work. Keep the shoulders down away from the ears; think of it as motor-pattern rehearsal for the loaded version."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bodyweight-overhead-press", "Bodyweight Overhead Press", "Front Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bodyweight overhead press stands and presses the arms overhead with no load, rehearsing the shoulder press pattern in warm-ups and mobility work. Keep the ribs down and reach tall at the top; it is a pattern primer, not a strength builder."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bodyweight-reverse-lunge", "Bodyweight Reverse Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The reverse lunge is a lower-body exercise targeting the quads, glutes, and hamstrings. The reverse lunge places more emphasis on the glutes than other lunge variations."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bodyweight-squat", "Bodyweight Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The bodyweight squat is a fundamental exercise that strengthens the quads, glutes, and hamstrings. It can be performed as part of a dynamic warm-up, for time or reps during a workout, or for hip and spine mobility any time."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bow-pose", "Bow Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Bow pose lies face-down, bends the knees and reaches back to grip the ankles, lifting the chest and thighs into an arch. It stretches the front of the body while strengthening the spinal erectors; keep the knees hip-width and avoid forcing the lift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("box-jump", "Box Jump", "Quadriceps", "Other", "plyometric_explosive", "compound", "The box jump is an explosive exercise that strengthens the muscles of the lower body, including the quads, glutes, calves, and hamstrings. They also develop power in the lower body while revving the heart rate. Box jumps can be performed for low reps to focus on strength and power, or for higher reps for metabolic conditioning. Most coaches recommend stepping down rather than jumping down between reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("box-squat", "Box Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The box squat squats back onto a box or bench, touching it briefly before driving up, which sets the depth and teaches the hip hinge. Sit back fully, keep the shins near vertical and stay braced through the touch; drive up without rocking forward."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bulgarian-split-squat", "Bulgarian Split Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell Bulgarian split squat is a single-leg exercise targeting the quads, glutes, and hamstrings. It is easier to overload with the barbell than when using dumbbells. If balance is an issue, consider using a Smith machine or holding dumbbells at your side instead."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-bulgarian-split-squat", "Bulgarian Split Squat (Kettlebell)", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell Bulgarian split squat loads a rear-foot-elevated split squat with one or two bells held at the chest or at the sides. The kettlebell's pull keeps the torso honest; sink the back knee and drive up through the front heel."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-bulgarian-split", "Bulgarian Split Squat (Smith Machine)", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine Bulgarian split squat sets the rear foot on a bench with the bar guiding a perfectly vertical path, letting the front leg take all the load. The fixed bar removes balance from the equation, so more load reaches the quads and glutes of the working leg."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("burpees", "Burpees", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The burpee is a high-intensity exercise that recruits the entire body to build strength and aerobic capacity. It is common in CrossFit workouts and group fitness classes, but can also be performed for time or reps in any fat-loss or athleticism-focused circuit or workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("butterfly-stretch", "Butterfly Stretch", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The butterfly stretch sits with the soles of the feet together and lets the knees fall toward the floor, opening the inner thighs and groin. Keep the spine long and hinge slightly from the hips rather than rounding; pressing gently on the knees deepens the stretch, and never bounce."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-bent-over-row", "Cable Bent-Over Row", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The cable bent-over row hinges forward and rows a cable attachment to the lower ribs, pulling against constant resistance through the whole range. The cable's line differs from a barbell, so the lats and mid-back work without lower-back compression; keep the torso steady and the elbows driving back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-chest-press", "Cable Chest Press", "Pectorals", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The cable chest press presses two handles forward from the chest from a standing or seated stance. The cables pull slightly apart through the press, so the pecs stabilize as well as push; step into a staggered stance and keep the wrists stacked over the elbows."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-crunch", "Cable Crunch", "Abs", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kneeling cable crunch is a popular core isolation exercise using a rope attachment and cable stack while kneeling on the floor. It primarily targets the rectus abdominus or \"six-pack\" muscles, but also strengthens the deep core muscles. It is most commonly performed early in an ab workout, usually in moderate to high reps like 8-12 reps per set or higher."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-curl", "Cable Curl", "Biceps", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable curl curls a handle or bar from a low pulley, keeping tension on the biceps at both the top and the bottom of every rep. Keep the elbows pinned to the sides and resist the weight on the way down; the constant load is the point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-external-rotation", "Cable External Rotation", "Rear Deltoids", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The cable external rotation rotates the upper arm outward against a low cable with the elbow pinned at the side, training the rotator cuff directly. Go light, since this is a precision drill rather than a strength showcase; control the return slowly."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("face-pull", "Cable Face Pull", "Rear Deltoids", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The standing face pull is an isolation exercise primarily targeting the rear deltoids, traps, and upper back. It is most often performed with a rope attachment."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-fly", "Cable Fly", "Pectorals", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable fly sweeps two pulley handles together in a wide arc in front of the chest, loading the pecs through constant tension. Keep a soft, fixed elbow bend and let the arms draw the arc from the shoulders; squeeze the hands together at the front for a beat."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-front-raise", "Cable Front Raise", "Front Deltoids", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The lying cable front raise is an isolation exercise for the shoulders, particularly the front deltoids. It is performed lying on the back, either on a bench or the ground, with the cable lifted over the torso. This movement is usually performed for moderate to high reps for a burn and pump as part of a shoulder-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-kickback", "Cable Glute Kickback", "Glutes", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable glute kickback extends the leg straight back against an ankle cuff from a low pulley, isolating the glute of the working hip. Keep the torso square to the machine; the temptation to arch is what the drill trains away. Squeeze the glute to finish, not the lower back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-hammer-curl", "Cable Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable rope hammer curl is a popular arm-focused exercise performed with a rope handle attached by a cable to a weight stack. It uses a neutral (palms facing one another) grip, which targets the forearms and brachialis muscles in addition to the biceps. Because grip is a limitation, it is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-lateral-raise", "Cable Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable lateral raise raises one arm out to the side against a low pulley, loading the side delt with tension from the very first degree. Keep the elbow slightly bent and the torso still; the cable's constant pull makes lighter weights feel honest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-pallof-press", "Cable Pallof Press", "Obliques", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The pallof press is an anti-rotation, isometric exercise that trains core stability. It is most often seen in programs for athletes who compete in sports that test strength, power, and functional movements."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-tricep-kickback", "Cable Tricep Kickback", "Triceps", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable tricep kickback hinges forward and extends the forearm back against a low cable, keeping tension on the triceps through the entire arc. Pin the upper arm parallel to the floor and let only the forearm move; the cable fixes the sagging resistance of the dumbbell version."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("tricep-pushdown", "Cable Tricep Pushdown", "Triceps", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable tricep pushdown extends the arms down against a high pulley, the staple triceps isolation with constant resistance. Pin the elbows to the ribs and split the hands slightly at the bottom; control the return rather than letting the stack fall."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-upright-row", "Cable Upright Row", "Lateral Deltoids", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The cable upright row pulls a bar or rope up the front of the body from a low pulley, training the side delts and upper traps. Keep the hands no wider than the shoulders and stop at chest height; the cable keeps the load smooth where a barbell jars."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cable-wrist-curl", "Cable Wrist Curl", "Forearms", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cable wrist curl flexes the wrists against a low cable with the forearms braced on the thighs or a bench. Constant tension makes even light weights effective; use full range and high reps for the flexors."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-calf-raise", "Calf Raise (Smith Machine)", "Calves", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The Smith machine standing calf raise is a machine-based exercise targeting the muscles of the calves, particularly the gastrocnemius or upper calf muscle. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of the lower-body portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("camel-pose", "Camel Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Camel pose kneels upright and reaches back to the heels, opening the chest and shoulders into a deep backbend. Press the hips forward over the knees and keep the glutes engaged so the bend comes from the upper back rather than hinging at the lumbar spine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("captains-chair-knee-raise", "Captain's Chair Knee Raise", "Abs", "Machine", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The captain's chair knee raise is a bodyweight exercise that targets the abs. It is performed on an apparatus that allows the forearms to rest on pads and the upright torso to hang freely. Knee raises are often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or more, as part of ab-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("captains-chair-leg-raise", "Captain's Chair Leg Raise", "Abs", "Machine", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The captain's chair leg raise is a bodyweight exercise that targets the abs. It is performed on an apparatus that allows the forearms to rest on pads and the upright torso to hang freely. Leg raise variations are often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or more, as part of ab-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cat-stretch", "Cat Stretch", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The cat stretch rounds and arches the spine on all fours in a slow, controlled rhythm. It mobilizes the whole vertebral column and re-establishes awareness of the core; sync each movement with a full breath cycle."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cat-cow", "Cat-Cow", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Cat-cow is a traditional yoga pose that is also popular in fitness and strength training. It targets spinal mobility and control of the vertebral column. It can be performed for time or reps as part of a dynamic warm-up or as a mobility or restorative exercise anytime. It can also work as part of a yoga or bodyweight sequence with other poses."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chair-pose", "Chair Pose", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Chair pose holds a deep quarter squat with the arms overhead, loading the quads, glutes and shoulders isometrically. Sit the hips back, keep the weight in the heels and draw the lower ribs in so the back stays long."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cheat-curl", "Cheat Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cheat curl uses a controlled body swing to start a heavier-than-strict barbell curl, then fights the lowering with pure biceps. The cheat is the setup, not the rep, and the eccentric must stay strict; it is an advanced overload tool, not a permission slip to swing every curl."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dips", "Chest Dips", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The chest dip is a chest-building exercise in which the lifter hangs between two parallel handles. Leaning the torso slightly forward shifts the emphasis from the triceps to the chest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chest-supported-dumbbell-shrug", "Chest Supported Dumbbell Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The chest-supported dumbbell shrug shrugs two heavy bells from a chest-down position on an incline bench, isolating the traps without the lower back in the equation. Keep the arms long and lift the shoulders toward the ears; a brief hold at the top does the work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chest-supported-db-row", "Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row", "Lats", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The chest-supported dumbbell row rows two bells from an incline bench with the chest pinned to the pad, removing every trace of body English. Pull the elbows past the ribs and squeeze the shoulder blades; the bells hang fully at the bottom of each rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chest-supported-kettlebell-row", "Chest-Supported Kettlebell Row", "Lats", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The chest-supported kettlebell row rows two bells from an incline bench with the chest pinned, taking all momentum out of the pull. Squeeze the shoulder blades together at the top and let the arms hang fully at the bottom; the back, not the arms, should ache after."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chest-supported-smith-machine-row", "Chest-Supported Smith Machine Row", "Lats", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The chest-supported Smith machine row sets an incline bench under the bar so the chest stays pinned while the arms row. The pad removes all cheating from the pull; squeeze the shoulder blades at the top and let the elbows drive past the ribs."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("childs-pose", "Child's Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Child’s pose is a traditional yoga pose that is also popular in fitness and strength training. It targets mobility of the thoracic spine and control of the vertebral column. It can be performed for time or reps as part of a dynamic warm-up or as a mobility or restorative exercise anytime. It can also work as part of a yoga or bodyweight sequence with other poses."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chin-tuck-hold", "Chin Tuck Hold", "Upper Traps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The chin tuck hold lies on the back and gently draws the chin toward the throat, holding the position to strengthen the deep neck flexors. It is a common corrective for forward-head posture and is usually held for 15 to 30 seconds at a time."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chin-ups", "Chin-Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The a chin-up is a variation of the pull-up exercise in which the reps are performed with the palms facing toward the body, in an underhand position, with a grip that is narrower than shoulder-width. Like other pull-up variations, it builds strength and muscle in the upper back, biceps, and core, but it utilizes the biceps slightly more than overhand pull-ups. It can be used as a more shoulder-friendly alternative to straight-bar pull-ups, or to help perform more reps than you can perform overhand."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("clamshells-hold", "Clamshell Hold", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The clamshell hold keeps the top knee of a side-lying clamshell open and suspended, turning the exercise into an isometric for the hip abductors. The leg should stay light; if the pelvis rocks backward, the glute is no longer doing the work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("clamshells", "Clamshells", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The clamshell lies on the side with the heels together and opens the top knee like a shell, training the glute medius through its smallest but most important job. Keep the pelvis perfectly still; if the hip rolls back, the glute has handed off the work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("clap-push-ups", "Clap Push-Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The clap push-up presses up explosively enough for the hands to leave the floor and clap before the next rep, developing upper-body power. Land with soft elbows and keep the body in one plank throughout; fall back to regular push-ups the moment the landings turn sloppy."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("clean", "Clean", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The clean pulls a barbell from the floor to the shoulders in one explosive motion, catching it on the front delts in a quarter squat. It develops triple-extension power through the hips, knees and ankles unlike any slow lift; the pull is vertical and the elbows whip under fast."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("clean-and-jerk", "Clean and Jerk", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The clean and jerk is a full-body lift that is one of two contested lifts in Olympic weightlifting competitions. It is comprised of two movements: the clean, where the bar is pulled from the floor and caught in the front rack position in three pulls or phases, followed by the jerk overhead. The clean and jerk hits every muscle from head to toe and can be the centerpiece of a lifting program. It is also popular in CrossFit and athletic training programs. It is usually performed in low rep ranges, focusing on power and movement quality."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-lat-pulldown", "Close Grip Lat Pulldown", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-grip pull-down is a variation on the lat pull-down, acable-based exercise machine that’s ubiquitous in gyms around the world. This back builder is easy to learn and highly effective, allowing for variable resistance depending on where you place the pin in the stack."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-push-ups", "Close Grip Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The close-grip push up keeps the hands directly under the shoulders or narrower, shifting the load from the chest toward the triceps. Keep the elbows brushing the ribs on the way down; the body stays in one line exactly as in a regular push up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-barbell-curl", "Close-Grip Barbell Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The close-grip barbell curl is a curl variation where the hands are closer than shoulder width. It is thought that this helps build the outer biceps head and the peak. This curl variation is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-bench-press", "Close-Grip Bench Press", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-grip bench press is a popular exercise targeting the triceps and chest. The main difference between this exercise and the standard bench press is that the hands and elbows are placed closer together, which places more of an emphasis on the triceps. The close-grip bench press is often used as an accessory movement for the traditional bench press, or as a triceps builder for arm training. Similar to the bench press, it can be trained in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle or strength endurance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-db-bench-press", "Close-Grip Dumbbell Bench Press", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-grip dumbbell bench press is a popular variation of the dumbbell bench press in which the dumbbells are held closer than shoulder width. This places more emphasis on the triceps, and perhaps also the inner chest. The close-grip dumbbell bench press can be performed in lower reps for strength or higher reps for muscle growth."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-ez-bar-bench-press", "Close-Grip EZ-Bar Bench Press", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-grip EZ-bar bench press is an exercise targeting the chest, shoulders, and triceps. This move is more commonly done with a straight bar and is often only performed with an EZ-bar as part of a complex of movements such as pull-overs or skullcrushers."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-ez-bar-curl", "Close-Grip EZ-Bar Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The close-grip EZ-bar curl is an exercise targeting the biceps, particularly the long head. The main difference between this exercise and standard EZ-bar curls is that the hands are positioned closer together on the bar. It is thought that this helps build the outer biceps head and the peak. This curl variation is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-close-grip-floor-press", "Close-Grip Floor Press (Kettlebell)", "Triceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell close-grip floor press lies on the floor and presses two bells with the elbows tucked close to the ribs. The floor stops the elbow safely and the narrow path loads the triceps hard; pause when the upper arms settle before the next rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-incline-bench", "Close-Grip Incline Bench Press", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-grip incline bench press presses from an incline bench with the hands inside shoulder width, stacking the triceps and upper chest together. Keep the elbows tucked close to the torso and touch the bar just below the collar line."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-grip-pull-ups", "Close-Grip Pull-Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-grip pull up hangs with the hands nearly touching and pulls the chest to the bar, shifting the load toward the lower lats and biceps. Keep the shoulders from shrugging and drive the elbows down toward the hips; the narrow grip asks more of the arms."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("close-stance-leg-press", "Close-Stance Leg Press", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The close-stance leg press places the feet hip-width and low on the platform, shifting the emphasis toward the quads. Keep the lower back glued to the pad and stop the set when the pelvis starts to tuck; depth is useful, lumbar rounding is not."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cobra-stretch", "Cobra Stretch", "Abs", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The cobra stretch lies face-down and presses the chest up with straight arms or a gentle lift, opening the abdominals and hip flexors. Keep the shoulders away from the ears and let the neck stay neutral; small doses after sitting or heavy squats feel best."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cocoons", "Cocoons", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The cocoon crunch is a bodyweight exercise targeting the ab muscles, the rectus abdominis or \"six-pack\" muscles in particular. It involves bringing your knees to your chest and your arms from overhead to center. It can be performed for time or reps as part of the ab-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("concentration-curl", "Concentration Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The concentration curl is a classic exercise for building the biceps one arm at a time. It can be performed bent over or kneeling, but is more often performed seated on a bench. It's great for emphasizing the biceps peak and is often used to finish a biceps workout. Like most curl variations, these are usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("savasana", "Corpse Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Corpse pose lies flat on the back with the limbs heavy and the breath slow. It closes a session by dropping the heart rate and releasing residual tension; the discipline is doing nothing deliberately, scanning and softening the body piece by piece."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cossack-squat", "Cossack Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The Cossack squat sinks sideways onto one loaded leg while the other leg extends out long, training the quads, adductors and ankle mobility at once. Keep the working heel down and the chest up; go only as deep as the hips and ankle allow without collapsing."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cow-face-pose", "Cow Face Pose", "Glutes", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Cow face pose stacks the knees and binds the hands behind the back, opening the outer hips and shoulders at the same time. Use a strap if the hands cannot meet, and switch sides to keep both hips even."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("crab-dips", "Crab Dips", "Triceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The crab dip lowers the body from a reverse tabletop position by bending the elbows, training the triceps through an unusual shoulder extension angle. Keep the hips high and the elbows tracking straight back; the shoulders should open, not shrug toward the ears."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("crescent-lunge", "Crescent Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The crescent lunge holds a deep forward lunge with the back knee lifted and the arms reaching overhead. It opens the hip flexors of the rear leg while the front leg and core hold the balance; keep the front knee stacked over the ankle."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cross-body-hammer-curl", "Cross Body Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The cross-body hammer curl is a dumbbell exercise that targets the biceps, brachialis, and forearm muscles. Rather than lifting the weights directly in front of the body, you lift them across the torso. Throughout each rep, the wrists remain neutral, like a carpenter hammering a nail. This helps isolate the brachialis muscles on each arm. It is generally performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or more, as part of the arm-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cross-body-crunch", "Cross-Body Crunch", "Obliques", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The elbow-to-knee crunch is a bodyweight exercise targeting the ab muscles, the obliques and rectus abdominis or \"six-pack\" muscles in particular. It can be performed for time or reps as part of the ab-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("cross-body-shoulder-stretch", "Cross-Body Shoulder Stretch", "Rear Deltoids", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The cross-body shoulder stretch targets increased flexibility in the shoulders. It is a common inclusion in upper-body dynamic warm-ups prior to training or sports, but can also be performed anytime to \"limber up\" the upper body."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("crow-pose", "Crow Pose", "Front Deltoids", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Crow pose balances the shins on the backs of the upper arms with the feet off the floor, training shoulder stability, wrist strength and core control. Start with one foot at a time or a block under the toes; look slightly forward, not straight down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("crunches", "Crunches", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The crunch is a popular core exercise targeting the rectus abdominis, or \"six-pack\" muscles, as well as the obliques. It has been the centerpiece of many ab-focused workouts, due to both its simplicity and the intense burn and mind-muscle connection it produces. It can be performed for time or reps as part of the ab-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dancer-pose", "Dancer Pose", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Dancer pose balances on one leg while holding the opposite foot behind the body, kicking the lifted leg into the hand to open the quad and shoulder. Fix the gaze on a point and hinge the torso forward gradually; it challenges balance as much as flexibility."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dead-bug", "Dead Bug", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The dead bug lies on the back and slowly extends the opposite arm and leg away from the trunk while the lower back stays pressed to the floor. It teaches the core to hold position while the limbs move; move slowly enough that nothing else on the body wobbles."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dead-bug-hold", "Dead Bug Hold", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The dead bug hold keeps the opposite arm and leg hovering off the floor in a static version of the dead bug. Press the lower back into the ground throughout; the moment it arches, the range is too long for the core to control."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dead-hang", "Dead Hang", "Forearms", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The dead hang simply hangs from a pull-up bar with straight arms, building grip strength, shoulder health and the lat mobility needed for pulling work. Let the shoulders rise toward the ears at the start and settle into a relaxed, active hang."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("decline-bench-press-barbell", "Decline Barbell Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The decline barbell bench press is a popular upper-body exercise targeting the lower pectoral muscles. While it can be a strength-focused movement, it is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of upper-body or chest-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("decline-bench-press", "Decline Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The decline barbell bench press is a popular upper-body exercise targeting the lower pectoral muscles. While it can be a strength-focused movement, it is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of upper-body or chest-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-decline-bench-press", "Decline Bench Press (Smith Machine)", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine decline bench press is a machine-based exercise targeting the chest muscles, particularly the lower pectoral muscles. The Smith machine mimics a barbell but provides more stability. This movement is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a chest or upper-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("decline-crunch", "Decline Crunch", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The decline crunch is an effective core exercise targeting the rectus abdominis."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("decline-db-fly", "Decline Dumbbell Fly", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The decline dumbbell chest fly is an upper body isolation exercise targeting the lower chest. It will require less weight than a decline press, which makes it a great hypertrophy exercise with high reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("decline-bench-press-ez-bar", "Decline EZ-Bar Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The decline EZ-bar bench press presses from a decline bench with the angled bar, biasing the lower chest while easing shoulder strain. Keep the feet hooked, the bar path over the lower chest and the elbows about 45 degrees from the torso."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("decline-push-up", "Decline Push-Up", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The feet-elevated push-up is a variation on the push-up, a body-weight standard. The feet are elevated, so that your body is aligned at an angle to the floor rather than parallel."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("deficit-deadlift", "Deficit Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell deficit deadlift is a compound exercise targeting the posterior chain."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("deficit-push-ups", "Deficit Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The deficit push up places the hands on blocks or dumbbells, letting the chest sink below hand level for a longer range. Keep the body in one line through the deeper bottom; the extra stretch is the growth, and the reason the reps get hard fast."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("diamond-push-ups", "Diamond Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The diamond push-up is an advanced variation of the push-up exercise performed with the hands in a diamond shape. It increases the challenge to the triceps, but also targets the chest and shoulders."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dolphin-pose", "Dolphin Pose", "Front Deltoids", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Dolphin pose sets up like downward dog but rests on the forearms, shifting the load into the shoulders and upper back. It is the standard strength drill behind headstand and forearm-stand progressions; press the floor away and keep the ribs knitted in."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("donkey-calf-raise", "Donkey Calf Raise", "Calves", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The donkey calk raise is a strength and muscle-building exercise focused on the muscles of the lower leg, most prominently the gastrocnemius muscle. While traditional donkey calf raises involved either a specific machine or carrying another person on one's back, this method uses a weight belt. It can be done in traditional muscle-building rep ranges, or for higher reps as part of lower-body training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("doorway-chest-stretch", "Doorway Chest Stretch", "Pectorals", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The doorway chest stretch places the forearms on a door frame and steps through until the chest opens. Elbow height decides the emphasis, lower for the lower chest and higher for the shoulders, so it is a quick, equipment-light fix for rounded posture."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-db-kickstand-deadlift", "Double Dumbbell Kickstand Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double dumbbell kickstand deadlift hinges with two bells and one foot's toe kicked back behind the other, a hybrid between bilateral and single-leg work. The rear toe is a balance point only; load the front leg, push the hips back and keep both bells hugging the shins."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-db-overhead-carry", "Double Dumbbell Overhead Carry", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The double dumbbell overhead carry walks with a bell locked out in each hand, pressing the shoulders and core to hold the ribcage down under two loads. Keep the biceps near the ears and the steps short; the carry ends when the lockout starts to drift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-bicep-curl", "Double Kettlebell Bicep Curl", "Biceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The double kettlebell bicep curl curls two bells together or alternately, front-loading the frame and doubling the grip demand. Keep the elbows pinned and the wrists neutral; the racked start position is half the core workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-clean", "Double Kettlebell Clean", "Glutes", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double kettlebell clean drives two bells from the swing into the rack in one hip-powered motion. It is the gateway to double presses and front squats; punch the hands through rather than muscling the bells around the wrists."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-clean-and-press", "Double Kettlebell Clean and Press", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double kettlebell clean and press chains a clean of both bells into a strict overhead press of both, then returns them under control. It is a brutal full-body strength and conditioning lift; every rep should start and end with crisp rack positions."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-dead-clean", "Double Kettlebell Dead Clean", "Glutes", "Kettlebell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The double kettlebell dead clean starts with both bells dead on the floor and cleans them to the rack in one pull. Starting from a dead stop removes the swing, so the pull is pure hip power; reset the setup each rep for the strictest version."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-dead-split-snatch", "Double Kettlebell Dead Split Snatch", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The double kettlebell dead split snatch pulls two dead bells from the floor and drives them overhead with a split stance catch. It is an advanced, explosive full-body lift; master the double clean and the split jerk separately before blending them."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-jerk", "Double Kettlebell Jerk", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double kettlebell jerk drives two racked bells overhead with a short dip and a punch of the legs, catching with straight arms. It moves far more load than the strict press and taxes the whole body; keep the dip shallow and the catch fast."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-overhead-carry", "Double Kettlebell Overhead Carry", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The double kettlebell overhead carry walks with a bell locked out in each hand, doubling the stability demand of the single version. The core must hold the ribcage down while the shoulders hold the lockout; short, frequent sets beat sloppy long ones."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-overhead-press", "Double Kettlebell Overhead Press", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double kettlebell overhead press presses two racked bells strictly overhead, loading the shoulders, triceps and trunk symmetrically. Keep the wrists stacked and the ribs down; the legs are quiet, as this is a press, not a push."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-push-press", "Double Kettlebell Push Press", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double-kettlebell push-press is a popular kettlebell movement that involves performing a press with help from the muscles of the lower body. It is preceded by a double clean at the start of each set, and optionally you can perform a clean between each rep. It can be trained in traditional strength or muscle-building rep ranges, in circuit or fat-loss training, or as part of a larger kettlebell combination or complex."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-rear-delt-row", "Double Kettlebell Rear Delt Row", "Rear Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double kettlebell rear delt row hinges forward and rows two bells high toward the chest with the elbows flared. The wide pull biases the rear delts and upper back; keep the torso close to parallel and the neck long."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-row", "Double Kettlebell Row", "Lats", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The double kettlebell row hinges and rows two bells to the ribs together, loading the lats and grip symmetrically. Keep the back flat and let the hips and core hold the hinge; the bells' pull makes balance part of the exercise."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-split-jerk", "Double Kettlebell Split Jerk", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The double kettlebell split jerk drives two racked bells overhead with a dip and a split-leg catch, the most load the overhead press family can move. Drop under the bells rather than pressing them up; recover the feet in order: front, back, together."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("double-kettlebell-swing-snatch", "Double Kettlebell Swing Snatch", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The double kettlebell swing snatch sends two swinging bells overhead in one violent, hip-driven motion. It is among the most demanding kettlebell lifts; own the double swing and double clean first, and expect the grip to give out before the hips do."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog-knee-tuck", "Downward Dog Knee Tuck", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The downward dog knee tuck pulls one knee toward the chest from a downward dog, blending a hamstring stretch with a core crunch. Keep the shoulders stacked over the wrists and round the spine as the knee draws in; alternate sides with control."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog-pedal", "Downward Dog Pedal", "Calves", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The downward dog pedal bends one knee while straightening the other in a slow alternating rhythm, warming the calves and hamstrings inside the downward dog position. It is a staple early stretch before lower-body training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog-to-knee-drive", "Downward Dog to Knee Drive", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The downward dog to knee drive pulls one knee toward the opposite elbow from a downward dog, blending a rotation crunch with a hamstring stretch of the standing leg. Keep the shoulders stacked over the wrists and let the spine round as the knee drives across."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog-to-low-lunge", "Downward Dog to Low Lunge", "Abs", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "This flow steps one foot from downward dog into a low lunge and back, opening the hip flexors of the trailing leg and the hamstrings of the leading one. Move with the breath and keep the transitions smooth; it is an ideal warm-up sequence before squats or runs."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog-to-plank", "Downward Dog to Plank", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The downward dog to plank flow rocks between the inverted V and the top of a push-up, training shoulder stability and core control in one smooth line. Keep the ribs knitted and the shoulders away from the ears in both positions; move with the breath."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog-to-upward-dog", "Downward Dog to Upward Dog", "Front Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The downward dog to upward dog flow sweeps from the inverted V through a low push-up into the chest-open backbend and back. Keep the shoulders down and the thighs hovering in the upward phase; it trains shoulder strength and spine extension together."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("downward-dog", "Downward-Facing Dog", "Calves", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Downward-facing dog forms an inverted V with the body, stretching the calves and hamstrings while the shoulders and arms bear weight. Press the floor away, reach the hips back and let the heels drift toward the ground rather than forcing them down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("drag-curl", "Drag Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The drag curl is a biceps exercise that attempts to remove the influence of the front deltoids on the classic curl. With the elbows kept back, the barbell \"drags\" up the torso. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-15 reps per set, as part of an upper-body or arms-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dragon-flag", "Dragon Flag", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The dragon flag lies on a bench or the floor, grips behind the head and lowers the straight body from a vertical position while keeping the hips and legs in one rigid line. It is one of the hardest core exercises there is; tuck the knees first and straighten them over weeks, controlling every degree of the descent."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-bench-press", "Dumbbell Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell bench press is a mainstay of workout enthusiasts worldwide. It’s a classic move for building a bigger, stronger chest. As such, it’s often placed first in mass-building chest workouts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-bench-pull", "Dumbbell Bench Pull", "Lats", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell bench pull lies chest-down on a high bench and rows a bell in each hand from below, pulling them to the underside of the bench. The pad pins the torso so the back does all the work; lower the bells under full control each rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("bicep-curl", "Dumbbell Bicep Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell biceps curl is a single-joint exercise for building bigger and stronger biceps. Popular among gym goers of all experience levels, this move can be done seated or standing. It is generally performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or higher, as part of the arm-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-calf-raise", "Dumbbell Calf Raise", "Calves", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell walking calf raise is a dynamic exercise targeting the calf muscles of the lower leg, particularly the gastrocnemius muscle. It can be performed for distance or for reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-deadlift", "Dumbbell Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell deadlift is a movement that targets the hamstrings, glutes, and back muscles. While it can be performed all the way from the ground, in the style of a barbell deadlift, it is more often performed from the top down as a Romanian deadlift. The dumbbell deadlift can be performed in low rep ranges to build posterior strength, or for moderate to high reps to build muscle and endurance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-face-pull", "Dumbbell Face Pull", "Rear Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell face pull hinges forward and pulls two bells high toward the face with the elbows wide, training the rear delts and the external rotators. Keep the torso still and finish with the elbows above the wrists; light weights and strict paths are the point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-farmers-walk", "Dumbbell Farmer's Walk", "Forearms", "Dumbbell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The dumbbell farmer's walk is an exercise with roots in competitive strongman training, but which is also popular in CrossFit and functional training. It can be a challenging finisher to any workout, or work well in fat-loss circuit training or as an accessory movement to heavy lifts like the deadlift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-floor-press", "Dumbbell Floor Press", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell floor press is a multijoint pressing exercise performed lying on the floor. It can be performed either with the knees bent or flat. The floor press limits the range of motion you would achieve with a regular dumbbell bench press, but still targets the chest, triceps, and anterior delts. This allows you to press more weight than in a dumbbell bench press, and perhaps put less stress on the shoulders."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-fly", "Dumbbell Fly", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell chest fly is a popular exercise that targets the pectoral or chest muscles. It attempts to isolate the chest muscles, stretching and contracting them while limiting involvement of the shoulders. Unlike a press, where the elbows bend and flex, flyes should keep a relatively consistent elbow angle. The motion is sometimes compared to \"hugging a tree.\" Dumbbell flyes are usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a chest or upper-body focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-front-raise", "Dumbbell Front Raise", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell front raise is a single-joint exercise targeting the shoulder muscles, particularly the front or anterior deltoids. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, at least 8-12 reps per set, as part of an upper-body or shoulder-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-front-squat", "Dumbbell Front Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell front squat is a popular exercise for targeting the muscles of the lower body, including the quadriceps, glutes, and hamstrings. Holding dumbbells in the racked position also works the core and upper-back muscles to maintain posture while the lower body moves. Because the amount of weight you can use will be limited by what you can clean to the rack position, this movement is usually performed for moderate to high reps, at least 8-12 reps per set or more. If this movement hurts your knees, try performing it with a wider stance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hammer-curl", "Dumbbell Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "This curl is performed simultaneously with dumbbells but without wrist supination. Throughout each rep, the wrists remain neutral, like a carpenter hammering a nail."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-hip-thrust", "Dumbbell Hip Thrust", "Glutes", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell hip thrust drives the hips to full extension with a bell across the pelvis, a low-friction alternative to the barbell version. Squeeze the glutes at the top and keep the chin tucked; pad the bell on the hip bone for comfort."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-kickstand-deadlift", "Dumbbell Kickstand Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell kickstand deadlift is a movement targeting the muscles of the hamstrings, glutes, and back. The kickstand position can help focus the stretch and contraction on each individual leg, similar to a single-leg Romanian deadlift, while presenting less of a balance challenge. It can be performed in low reps, such as 5-8 reps per set, for strengthening the posterior chain, or for higher reps for muscle growth."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("lateral-raise", "Dumbbell Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell lateral raise is a shoulder exercise that targets the medial or middle head of the deltoid muscle. It's a staple strength-training move and is a great option for accessory work on upper-body training days. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, at least 8-12 reps, as part of the upper-body or shoulder-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-lunge", "Dumbbell Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell split squat is a popular lower-body exercise to build strength and muscle one leg at a time. It can be used to teach proper lunge form, but is also valuable on its own when trained in traditional strength-focused rep ranges, such as 5-8 reps per set, or for higher reps to build muscle or for conditioning."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-overhead-carry", "Dumbbell Overhead Carry", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The dumbbell overhead carry walks with one bell locked out overhead, training shoulder stability and anti-side-bend core strength at once. Keep the bell stacked over the shoulder and the hip and the ribs down; switch sides at the halfway point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-pistol-squat", "Dumbbell Pistol Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell pistol squat holds a bell at the chest as counterweight while squatting to a single leg, making the balance point easier to find. Keep the standing heel down and the free leg long; the bell reaches forward as the hips sink back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-pullover", "Dumbbell Pullover", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell pull-over is a popular muscle and strength-building exercise that targets the muscles of the chest, upper back, lats, and the serratus anterior. It was a favorite of old-school bodybuilders, who believed high-rep pull-overs helped build a V-taper shape in the torso."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-push-press", "Dumbbell Push Press", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell push press dips the knees slightly and drives two bells overhead with leg power, allowing heavier loads than the strict press. Time the dip and drive as one; the arms only finish the job the legs start."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-reverse-curl", "Dumbbell Reverse Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "With the reverse-grip dumbbell curl, the palms face down, not up. This unorthodox wrist position simultaneously targets the biceps while also building forearm and grip strength. The move can be performed standing or seated. Because grip is a limiting factor, it is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-10 reps per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-reverse-fly", "Dumbbell Reverse Fly", "Rear Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The incline dumbbell reverse fly is an upper-body exercise targeting the posterior or rear deltoids, as well as the postural muscles of the upper back. Because it targets such small muscles, this exercise is usually performed with light weight for high reps, such as 10-15 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-romanian-deadlift", "Dumbbell Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell stiff-legged deadlift targets the hamstrings, glutes, low and upper back, as well as the core. The purpose of the stiff-legged, as opposed to Romanian deadlift with slightly bent knees, is to engage the hamstrings and low back to an even greater degree."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-shoulder-press", "Dumbbell Shoulder Press", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The standing dumbbell shoulder press is a classic deltoid-builder (especially the front deltoids) that’s ubiquitous in gyms around the world. Performing the move standing up as opposed to seated allows for more weight to be used."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-shrug", "Dumbbell Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The standing dumbbell shrug is a popular movement to build and strengthen the trapezius muscles. It’s a often trained on a bodybuilding-style shoulder or back training day, but can also work well in full-body or strength-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-side-bend", "Dumbbell Side Bend", "Obliques", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell side bend is a popular exercise for targeting the oblique muscles of the abdomen. It is usually performed for relatively high reps, at least 8-12 reps per set or more. It can be performed one side at a time or alternating sides."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-skull-crusher", "Dumbbell Skull Crusher", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell skullcrusher is a triceps exercise that has been a staple of bodybuilders for decades. Skullcrushers are usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of an upper-body or arm-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-snatch", "Dumbbell Snatch", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The dumbbell snatch drives a single bell from the floor or the hang to overhead in one explosive pull, catching it locked out with a dip. It is a fast, forgiving full-body power lift; keep the bell close on the way up and punch through at the top."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-somersault-squat", "Dumbbell Somersault Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell somersault squat holds a bell at the chest and squats while rolling the torso in a controlled forward circle, keeping constant tension on the quads through an honest range. Move slowly, so the circle feels like a controlled tilt rather than a wobble; the bell's front load keeps the spine tall."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-split-squat", "Dumbbell Split Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell split squat is a popular lower-body exercise to build strength and muscle one leg at a time. It can be used to teach proper lunge form, but is also valuable on its own when trained in traditional strength-focused rep ranges, such as 5-8 reps per set, or for higher reps to build muscle or for conditioning."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-squat", "Dumbbell Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell squat is a classic lower-body exercise that targets the quadriceps, hamstrings, and glutes. While this variation is usually performed with relatively light weights for high reps, it can also be used as a substitute for squats or the trap-bar deadlift in any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-sumo-squat", "Dumbbell Sumo Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell sumo squat is a popular lower-body movement using a single dumbbell held in front of the legs. It is performed with a wider than shoulder-width stance, which increases the demand on the glutes, hamstrings, and adductors while still benefiting the quads, core, and upper body. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, at least 8-12 reps per set, as part of the lower-body portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-svend-press", "Dumbbell Svend Press", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell Svend press presses the top of a bell between both palms out from the chest, an isometric squeeze that lights up the inner pecs. Keep the squeeze relentless through the whole range; short range, high tension, high reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-tricep-extension", "Dumbbell Tricep Extension", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell tricep extension lowers a bell behind the head from an overhead grip, stretching the triceps' long head through its fullest range. Keep the elbows narrow and pointing forward; the deeper the stretch, the better the rep, within shoulder comfort."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("tricep-kickback", "Dumbbell Tricep Kickback", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single-arm triceps kick-back is an isolation move from a bent-over position used to increase size and strength of the triceps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-upright-row", "Dumbbell Upright Row", "Lateral Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The standing dumbbell upright row is a popular movement for building stronger and bigger traps and shoulders. Many lifters combine this move with either their back or shoulder workout, since it involves both body parts. It is generally performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-windmill", "Dumbbell Windmill", "Lateral Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The dumbbell windmill holds a bell overhead and hinges sideways to touch the floor with the free hand, keeping the eyes on the bell throughout. It stretches the hip and hamstring of one side while the shoulder stabilizes a load overhead; move slowly and keep the bell arm vertical."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("dumbbell-wrist-curl", "Dumbbell Wrist Curl", "Forearms", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell wrist curl curls the wrists up from a braced forearm position, training the flexors one or two hands at a time. Full range matters more than load; let the bell roll down to the fingertips, then curl it back with the fingers and wrist together."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("eagle-pose", "Eagle Pose", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Eagle pose wraps one leg over the other and binds the arms in front of the chest, holding the tangled balance. It stretches the outer hips and between the shoulder blades while demanding steady single-leg control; swap the wrap direction to even both sides."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("easy-pose", "Easy Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Easy pose is a simple seated cross-legged position used to settle posture and breathing. Sit tall on the sitting bones, let the knees drop toward the floor and alternate the leg crossing to keep the hips symmetrical."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("elliptical-trainer", "Elliptical Trainer", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The elliptical trainer is a popular machine for cardiovascular training without the significant impact on your joints of running. On many machines, you can adjust the resistance level to make the movement more difficult for all four limbs. The elliptical is a mainstay in most commercial gyms, and can be equally effective for HIIT-style interval workouts or for longer-duration endurance work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("extended-side-angle", "Extended Side Angle Pose", "Quadriceps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Extended side angle pose sinks into a wide lunge and lays the same-side forearm on the thigh, reaching the top arm over the ear to lengthen the whole side body. Keep the chest open toward the ceiling rather than collapsing forward."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-pullover", "EZ Bar Pullover", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The EZ-bar pullover arcs the angled bar from the chest to beyond the head while lying down, stretching the lats and ribcage under load. The angled grip spares the shoulders that a straight bar punishes; keep the hips planted and the arms softly bent."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-bench-press", "EZ-Bar Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The EZ-bar bench press benches with the angled bar, trading a little pec load for a far friendlier wrist and elbow position. Grip the inner bends, keep the forearms vertical at the bottom and drive the bar up in a slight arc over the chest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-curl", "EZ-Bar Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The standing EZ-bar curl is an immensely popular exercise targeting the biceps. It is commonly performed for moderate to high reps as part of an upper body or arms-focused workout. Many gyms even have EZ-bars at fixed weights, allowing for easy weight changes."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-front-raise", "EZ-Bar Front Raise", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar front raise lifts the angled bar from the thighs to shoulder height, training the front delts with a wrist-friendly grip. Keep the elbows nearly straight and the torso still; raise only to shoulder height, since momentum beyond that is wasted."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-reverse-curl", "EZ-Bar Reverse Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The reverse-grip EZ-bar curl is an exercise targeting the biceps and forearms. Because grip strength is a limitation with this move, it is often performed for moderate to high reps as part of an upper-body or arm-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-romanian-deadlift", "EZ-Bar Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The EZ-bar Romanian deadlift hinges the hips back under the angled bar, stretching the hamstrings to their working end and driving back up. The angled grip suits stiff wrists and lets the load sit closer; keep the bar grazing the legs and the spine long."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-shrug", "EZ-Bar Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The seated EZ-bar shrug is an exercise targeting the trapezius muscle, that V-shaped muscle that forms a triangle where the shoulders meet the neck. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps during an upper-body or shoulders and traps-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-upright-row", "EZ-Bar Upright Row", "Lateral Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The EZ-bar upright row pulls the angled bar up the front of the body to chest height, working the side delts and traps through a wrist-neutral grip. Keep the elbows above the hands and stop before the pull pinches the shoulder; moderate weight, strict path."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-wrist-curl", "EZ-Bar Wrist Curl", "Forearms", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar wrist curl flexes the wrists under the angled bar with the forearms braced, training the flexors through the most wrist-comfortable angle available. Full range beats heavy weight here: let the bar roll to the fingertips and curl it back up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("fish-pose", "Fish Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Fish pose lies on the back and arches the chest toward the ceiling with the crown of the head resting lightly on the floor. It opens the front of the neck, chest and shoulders after long sitting; keep the weight on the elbows, never on the head."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("floor-ez-bar-press", "Floor EZ-Bar Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The floor EZ-bar press benches from the floor, which stops the elbows at a safe depth while the angled bar spares the wrists. It hammers the lockout half of the press and the triceps; pause when the upper arms settle before driving up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("floor-kettlebell-pullover", "Floor Kettlebell Pullover", "Lats", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The floor kettlebell pullover arcs a bell from the chest to beyond the head and back while lying on the floor, stretching the lats under a light load. The floor keeps the ribs honest; stop the arc if the lower back leaves the ground."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("floor-press", "Floor Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The floor press presses a barbell from the floor with the shoulder blades pinned, cutting the range at the point the shoulders would take over. It builds raw triceps and lockout strength and spares cranky shoulders; let the triceps rest briefly on the floor between reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("flutter-kicks", "Flutter Kicks", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The flutter kick is a core exercise where the legs are held off the ground as you flutter your feet over and under each other in small motions. It targets multiple muscles of the core, but particularly the rectus abdominis or \"six-pack\" muscles. It can be performed for time or reps in the ab-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("front-lever", "Front Lever", "Lats", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The front lever hangs from a bar or rings and raises the straight body to horizontal, face-up. It is one of the most demanding straight-arm skills, loading the lats and core enormously; tuck, then advance through one-leg variations before the full hold."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("front-squat", "Front Squat", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The front squat squats with the bar racked on the front delts, keeping the torso upright and shifting load toward the quads and upper back. The elbows stay high and the wrists extended; it is the most technically honest of the heavy squats, and a weak position shows immediately."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-front-squat", "Front Squat (Smith Machine)", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine front squat is a machine-based exercise targeting the muscles of the lower body. The Smith machine mimics a barbell but provides more stability. This movement can be programmed in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle and strength endurance. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a full-body or lower-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("garland-pose", "Garland Pose", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Garland pose settles into a deep squat with the heels down and the elbows pressing the knees outward. It opens the adductors, ankles and hips and is an excellent diagnostic of squat mobility; elevate the heels slightly if they lift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("glute-bridge", "Glute Bridge", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The glute bridge is a popular lower-body bodyweight exercise that primarily targets the glutes, but also activates the lower back and hamstrings. It can be performed with body weight alone, or with added resistance such as barbells, dumbbells, or bands. It can be performed for time or reps and works perfectly in a dynamic warm-up for lower-body training, but can also be performed for glute activation anytime."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("glute-bridge-hold", "Glute Bridge Hold", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "isolation", "The glute bridge hold lifts the hips into full extension and keeps them there, squeezing the glutes continuously. Push through the heels, tuck the pelvis and stop short of arching the lower back; holds of 20 to 45 seconds build endurance in the posterior chain."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("glute-kickback", "Glute Kickback", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The glute kickback extends one leg straight back and up from a quadruped position, isolating the glute of the working hip. Keep the hips square to the floor, since the leg lifts by squeezing the glute, not by arching the back; small range, hard squeeze."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("glute-kickback-hold", "Glute Kickback Hold", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "isolation", "The glute kickback hold suspends a leg extended behind the body from a quadruped position, holding it against gravity. Keep the hips square to the floor; height matters less than a locked-in pelvis. It is a glute isolation drill rather than a leg lift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("goblet-squat", "Goblet Squat", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The goblet squat is a popular lower-body exercise where you hold a kettlebell or dumbbell in front of your chest as if you are supporting a heavy cup or goblet. It can help lifters of all levels improve their squat technique and hip mobility, but can also work as a muscle-building or strength accessory movement for experienced athletes. It also works well in combinations, complexes, and flows with other kettlebell movements like swings, presses, and cleans."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("good-morning", "Good Morning", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell good morning is an exercise that targets glute, hamstring, and lower back development. It is often trained as an accessory movement to the deadlift, but also has value on its own. It is sometimes performed for reps in traditional strength-focused rep ranges such as 5-8 reps per set, but due to the risk posed to the lower back, is rarely treated as a max-effort single-rep lift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-good-morning", "Good Morning (Smith Machine)", "Hamstrings", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine good morning hinges at the hips under a guided bar on the back, loading the hamstrings and erectors through a controlled bow. The fixed path makes the pattern safer to learn than the free-bar version; keep the knees soft and the spine long."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hack-squat", "Hack Squat", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The hack squat is a machine-based exercise that targets the muscles of the legs, particularly the quadriceps. It is often used as an accessory for squats, but can also be the main lower-body movement in a workout. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hack-squat-calf-raise", "Hack Squat Calf Raise", "Calves", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The hack squat calf raise is a variation of the standing calf raise performed in a hack squat machine. This allows for heavy loading, but also shoulder comfort from the pads of the hack squat. Calf raise variations are often performed for relatively high reps, such as 10-20 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("half-moon-pose", "Half Moon Pose", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Half moon pose balances on one leg with the torso tipped to horizontal and the free leg lifted to hip height or above. It trains balance and hip stability while opening the standing-side hamstring; a block under the lower hand keeps the spine long."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("half-kneeling-hip-flexor-rock", "Half-Kneeling Hip Flexor Rock", "Abs", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The half-kneeling hip flexor rock rocks the hips forward and back from a half-kneeling stance, rhythmically loading and releasing the front of the rear hip. Keep the glute of the rear leg squeezed and the ribs down; it is a controlled mobility drill, not a stretch-and-forget hold."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("handstand-push-ups", "Handstand Push Ups", "Front Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The handstand push-up is an exercise targeting the shoulders, although other muscles assist in the completion of the reps. It is common in both CrossFit workouts and advanced bodyweight or calisthenics training. Given the degree of difficulty, beginners will need to progress to this move."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hang-clean", "Hang Clean", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The hang clean pulls the bar from above the knee to the shoulders in one explosive extension, trading the floor pull for pure power from the hang. Keep the bar close as the hips extend, then whip the elbows under into the front rack."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hang-power-clean", "Hang Power Clean", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The hang power clean catches the bar above parallel in a shallow dip, pulling from the hang and landing tall. It is the most repeatable clean variation for power work; the same crisp extension with less demand on the front-rack mobility."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hanging-knee-raise", "Hanging Knee Raise", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The alternating hanging knee raise is an exercise targeting the abs, but which also works the lats and hip flexors. Rather than performing both legs at a time, you lift one knee at a time and alternate with each rep as if marching in place. Knee raises are often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or more, as part of ab-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hanging-leg-raise", "Hanging Leg Raise", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The hanging leg raise is an exercise targeting the abs, but which also works the lats and hip flexors. Instead of resting your forearms on the pads of a captain's chair, you perform these hanging from a bar. Experienced lifters make these look easy, but beginners may need time to build up to sets of 8-12 reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hanging-pike", "Hanging Pike", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The hanging pike hangs from a bar and raises the straight legs to hip height or above, piking the hips toward the bar. Keep the swing out of the rep: the hips curl, the legs do not simply swing. It sits between the knee raise and the toes-to-bar."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("happy-baby", "Happy Baby Pose", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Happy baby pose lies on the back, grips the outsides of the feet and draws the knees toward the armpits. It releases the adductors and lower back gently; rock side to side for a light spinal massage and keep the tailbone heavy on the floor."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("head-to-knee-pose", "Head-to-Knee Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Head-to-knee pose folds one straight leg toward the chest while hinging over the extended other leg. Keep the lengthened spine rather than chasing the shin with a rounded back; it stretches the hamstring and calf of the extended leg."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("heel-elevated-squat", "Heel-Elevated Squat", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The heel-elevated squat raises the heels on a plate or wedge and squats, letting the knees travel further forward to load the quads through a longer range. Keep the torso upright and sink between the feet; the depth the wedge buys is the point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("heel-to-toe-walk", "Heel-to-Toe Walk", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The heel-to-toe walk walks in a straight line placing the heel of one foot directly before the toes of the other, a balance drill that challenges the ankles and the stabilizing chain of the standing leg. Keep the arms at the sides and the gaze ahead, not down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hero-pose", "Hero Pose", "Quadriceps", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "Hero pose kneels between the feet with the calves alongside the thighs, stretching the quads and ankles deeply. Sit on a block if the hips cannot reach the floor and keep the spine upright; short holds are enough at first."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hex-bar-deadlift", "Hex Bar Deadlift", "Spinal Erectors", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The hex bar deadlift is a variety of deadlift performed in a hexagonal barbell also known as a trap bar. The bar is held with a parallel (palms in) grip outside of the legs. It is biomechanically different from a barbell deadlift in that it lines up with a lifter’s center of gravity, rather than placing it in front of their body. The trap bar deadlift can be trained in traditional strength and muscle-focused rep range, or for higher reps. It activates all of the same muscle groups as conventional or sumo deadlifts, including the glutes, quads, hamstring, lower back, lats, upper back, and grip."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("high-knees", "High Knees", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "High knees is a full-body, cardio-based exercise with an emphasis on the lower body. It is often performed for reps or time as part of a dynamic warm-up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("high-plank", "High Plank", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The high plank holds the top of a push-up with straight arms, stacking the shoulders over the wrists and bracing the whole trunk. Push the floor away, squeeze the glutes and keep the neck long; it is both a core drill and the setup for push-up variations."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("high-foot-leg-press", "High-Foot Leg Press", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The high-foot leg press places the feet high and wide on the platform so the hips and hamstrings do most of the pushing. The knees travel less and the glutes load hardest at depth; expect it to feel closer to a hinge than a squat."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hip-adduction", "Hip Adduction", "Hip Adductors", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine hip adduction squeezes the legs together against weighted pads, training the adductors directly. Keep the movement smooth through the full travel; the stretch at the wide position is where most of the value lives."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stability-ball-hip-bridge", "Hip Bridge (Ball)", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The stability ball hip bridge rests the feet on a stability ball and bridges the hips up, adding an unstable platform to the glute bridge. Keep the ball from drifting and the hips level; the hamstrings and glutes work overtime to steady the ball."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-hip-thrust", "Hip Thrust (Smith Machine)", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine hip thrust drives the hips to full extension against a bar that travels straight up over them. The fixed path removes the balancing act of the free-bar version, so the glutes can be loaded hard and progressively."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hollow-body-hold", "Hollow Body Hold", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The hollow-body hold is an isometric exercise that increases strength and stability in the core muscles. It is common in gymnastics training, CrossFit training, and as a core tension and bracing drill for lifters and athletes of all types. It is performed for time and can be performed as an activation drill prior to lifting, as part of a circuit, or as equipment-free core training anytime."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("horizontal-leg-press", "Horizontal Leg Press", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The horizontal leg press pushes a weighted sled along a rail from a reclined seat, loading the legs without compressing the spine. Keep the whole foot flat and the pelvis on the pad; the range should end before the hips curl off the seat."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("human-flag", "Human Flag", "Lats", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The human flag grips a vertical pole and holds the straight body sideways off it. It demands exceptional oblique and shoulder strength; the progression runs through tucked, straddle and negative versions long before the full flag."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-bench-press", "Incline Barbell Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The incline bench press is an upper body compound movement that targets the upper chest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-incline-bench-press", "Incline Bench Press (Smith Machine)", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine incline bench press is a machine-based movement targeting the chest, particularly the upper chest. The Smith machine mimics a barbell but provides more stability. This movement can be programmed in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle and strength endurance. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a chest or upper-body focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-db-curl", "Incline Dumbbell Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The incline dumbbell biceps curl is an isolation exercise targeting the biceps. This variation targets the long head of the biceps more than a traditional curl and there is more overall tension to this muscle."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-dumbbell-fly", "Incline Dumbbell Fly", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The incline dumbbell chest fly is an upper body isolation exercise targeting the upper chest and is typically used with aesthetic goals in mind. It will require less weight than an incline press, which makes it a great hypertrophy exercise with high reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-db-press", "Incline Dumbbell Press", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The incline dumbbell bench press is an upper body exercise targeting the upper pectoral muscles."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-bench-ez-bar-press", "Incline EZ-Bar Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The incline EZ-bar bench press presses from an incline bench with the angled bar, biasing the upper chest while easing wrist and elbow strain. Keep the bar path over the collar line, the elbows about 45 degrees from the torso and the shoulder blades pinched."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-hammer-curl", "Incline Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The incline hammer curl curls from an incline bench with the arms hanging behind the torso, putting the biceps under a long stretch while the neutral grip targets the brachialis. Keep the elbows still and let the arm hang fully at the bottom; the stretch is the growth zone."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-push-ups", "Incline Push-Up", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The hands-elevated push-up is a variation on the push-up, a body-weight standard. The hands are elevated, so that your body is aligned at an angle to the floor rather than parallel."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("incline-treadmill-walk", "Incline Treadmill Walk", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The incline treadmill walk sets the belt to a steep grade and walks at a steady pace, turning a low-impact stroll into a demanding conditioning tool. The glutes and calves do much of the work; resist the urge to grip the handrails, which unloads the legs."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("inverted-row", "Inverted Row", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine inverted row is a bodyweight exercise targeting the muscles of the lats (latissimus dorsi) and upper back, using the bar of the Smith machine to hang and pull from. You can change the bar height to customize the difficulty level and desired angle of the row. This variation is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("isometric-neck-side", "Isometric Neck Lateral Flexion", "Upper Traps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "isolation", "Isometric neck lateral flexion presses the hand against the side of the head while the neck resists sideways bending. Train both sides evenly and keep the pressure modest; steady holds build the neck strength that contact sports and heavy pulls demand."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("jackknife-sit-up", "Jackknife Sit-Up", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The jackknife sit-up raises the straight arms and legs together from the floor until the hands meet the shins overhead, folding the body in two. Keep the lower back on the floor and hinge at the hips while the ribs curl up; lower both ends slowly."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("jefferson-curl", "Jefferson Curl", "Spinal Erectors", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Jefferson curl rolls the spine down one vertebra at a time while holding a light weight, then unrolls back to standing, loading the entire back line through flexion and extension. Start nearly unloaded and move with extreme control; it is a mobility-strength drill for the spine and hamstrings, not a heavy lift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("jump-rope", "Jump Rope", "Calves", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "Jump rope turns a rope over the head and under the feet in continuous small hops, training the calves, shoulders and coordination while spiking the heart rate. Stay on the balls of the feet with low, quiet bounces; it scales from easy warm-up rounds to brutal intervals."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("jump-squat", "Jump Squat", "Quadriceps", "Other", "plyometric_explosive", "compound", "The jump squat is an explosive bodyweight exercise targeting the quads, hamstrings, and glutes. It is also a serious cardiovascular challenge when done for reps. It can be performed as a power exercise to build jumping power, or in any fat-loss or athleticism-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("jumping-jacks", "Jumping Jacks", "Calves", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The jumping jack is a classic calisthenics exercise to improve overall conditioning. It’s one the most recognizable exercises on the planet, and it’s almost synonymous with training for military recruits. It can be done for time or reps, often in dynamic warm-ups or to mobilize the hips and shoulders."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-concentration-curl", "Kettlebell Concentration Curl", "Biceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell concentration curl braces the working elbow against the inner thigh and curls a bell in strict isolation. The brace removes every shortcut; squeeze at the top and lower slowly under control."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-deadlift", "Kettlebell Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell deadlift is a foundational movement in kettlebell training. It's often taught as a buildup or regression to the kettlebell swing, as well as to instruct the \"hip hinge\" motion in a more approachable manner than with a barbell."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-farmers-walk", "Kettlebell Farmer's Walk", "Forearms", "Kettlebell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The kettlebell farmer's walk carries a heavy bell in each hand for distance or time. The grip, traps and core hold everything together while the legs walk; stay tall, keep the shoulders packed and take short, quick steps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-floor-press", "Kettlebell Floor Press", "Pectorals", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell alternating floor press limits the range of motion you would achieve with a dumbbell or kettlebell bench press, but still targets the chest, triceps, and anterior delts. This allows you to build strength in the top portion of the lift, overload the triceps, and work on strengthening the shoulder stabilizers."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-goblet-lunge", "Kettlebell Goblet Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell goblet lunge holds a bell at the chest through lunges, front-loading the torso so the core works as hard as the legs. The counterweight makes it easy to keep the spine tall; step or walk, and keep the front knee tracking over the shoe."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-halo", "Kettlebell Halo", "Lateral Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell halo circles a bell around the head at collarbone height, training shoulder mobility and core control. Keep the hips quiet, since the movement belongs to the shoulders and ribs; reverse directions halfway through each set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-hammer-curl", "Kettlebell Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell hammer curl curls the bell by its handle with the wrist neutral, biasing the brachialis and forearm. Keep the elbow pinned to the side and let the bell rotate naturally in the hand."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-hip-thrust", "Kettlebell Hip Thrust", "Glutes", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell hip thrust drives the hips to full extension with a bell padded across the pelvis. The single-bell setup needs no bar or bench changes; squeeze the glutes hard at the top and keep the chin tucked."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-kickstand-deadlift", "Kettlebell Kickstand Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell kickstand deadlift hinges with one foot's toe kicked back behind the other, splitting the difference between a bilateral and a single-leg pattern. The rear leg is a kickstand, not a worker; keep the weight over the front leg and push the hips back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-lunge-press", "Kettlebell Lunge Press", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell lunge press steps into a lunge and presses the bell overhead as the knee descends. The legs create instability that the pressing shoulder must absorb; alternate legs and keep the wrist stacked over the elbow."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-overhead-carry", "Kettlebell Overhead Carry", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The kettlebell overhead carry walks with a bell locked out overhead, training shoulder stability, core strength and grip in one drill. Keep the bicep near the ear and the ribcage stacked over the pelvis; switch hands at the halfway mark."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-pistol-squat", "Kettlebell Pistol Squat", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell pistol squat holds a bell at the chest as counterweight while squatting on one leg. The weight shifts the balance point backward, making the hardest part of the pistol, staying over the foot, far more manageable."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-pullover", "Kettlebell Pullover", "Lats", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell pullover arcs a bell from the chest to behind the head and back while lying down, stretching the lats and demanding core control. Keep the lower back pinned to the floor; the moment it arches, the weight is too heavy."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-reverse-lunge", "Kettlebell Reverse Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell reverse lunge steps back under load held at the chest or in the rack, sparing the knees while loading the quads and glutes. Keep the torso upright and let the front leg own the depth; the bell counterbalances the hinge."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-rotational-lunge", "Kettlebell Rotational Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell rotational lunge sweeps the bell across the body into a lunge, blending a lateral step, a rotation and a squat. Let the hips lead the turn and keep the arms relatively long; it is a favorite athletic warm-up and conditioner."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-russian-twist", "Kettlebell Russian Twist", "Obliques", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell Russian twist sits with the heels down and sweeps the bell side to side across the floor, rotating through the ribcage. Keep the chest tall rather than slumping; the harder the rotation, the less the arms should lead."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-shrug", "Kettlebell Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell shrug lifts the shoulders straight toward the ears against heavy bells, training the upper traps. Keep the arms long and the elbows soft; a one-second hold at the top beats momentum."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-skull-crusher", "Kettlebell Skull Crusher", "Triceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell skull crusher lowers the bells beside the head from a locked-out press and extends back up, isolating the triceps. The bell's center of mass pulls the hands slightly apart, which many elbows prefer over a straight bar."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-squat", "Kettlebell Squat", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell squat holds the bell at the chest through a full squat, front-loading the body so the core and upper back work with the legs. Keep the elbows inside the knees at the bottom and the chest proud throughout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-sumo-deadlift", "Kettlebell Sumo Deadlift", "Glutes", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell sumo deadlift pulls a heavy bell from between the feet in a wide stance, training the glutes, adductors and grip through a full hinge. Wedge the hips down to the bell, keep the shins vertical and stand tall by driving the floor away."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-svend-press", "Kettlebell Svend Press", "Pectorals", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell Svend press squeezes a bell flat between the palms and presses it straight out at chest height. The isometric squeeze is the exercise: the pecs work to hold the bell while the press adds range. Keep the elbows soft."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-swing", "Kettlebell Swing", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell swing is a popular lower-body exercise emphasizing the hamstrings, glutes, and back muscles. It is often used to train explosive power, for aerobic or cardiovascular conditioning, in circuit training, or as an accessory movement for the deadlift."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-swing-clean", "Kettlebell Swing Clean", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The kettlebell swing clean catches a swing-height bell into the rack position without the extra vertical of a full clean. It teaches the punch-through that all clean work relies on; let the hips throw the bell and the hand merely steers it."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-windmills", "Kettlebell Windmills", "Hip Adductors", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-kettlebell windmill is a dynamic kettlebell exercise emphasizing core strength and shoulder and hip mobility and stability. It is often used as a functional warm-up or for multi-directional strength work. It doesn't work as well in a metcon or conditioning setting, and shouldn't be performed under intense fatigue."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-wrist-curl", "Kettlebell Wrist Curl", "Forearms", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell wrist curl rests the forearms on the thighs and hinges the bell up with the fingers flexed, training the wrist flexors and grip. Use a light bell and a full range; the hands tolerate high reps here better than heavy grinds."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("knee-push-ups", "Knee Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The knee push up performs the push-up with the knees on the floor, cutting the load roughly in half while keeping the pressing pattern intact. Keep a straight line from the shoulders to the knees with no sagging hips; it is the standard on-ramp to full push ups."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stability-ball-knee-tuck", "Knee Tuck (Ball)", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The stability ball knee tuck pulls the knees toward the chest from a high plank with the feet on the ball, rolling the ball in with the core. Keep the shoulders stacked over the wrists and the hips from sagging; the ball should move because the abs curled, not because the arms pulled."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("knee-to-chest-stretch", "Knee-to-Chest Stretch", "Glutes", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The knee-to-chest stretch lies on the back and draws one or both knees in toward the chest. It decompresses the lower back and releases the glutes; keep the head relaxed on the floor and breathe into the stretch."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kneeling-cable-row", "Kneeling Cable Row", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kneeling cable row kneels facing a low pulley and rows the handle to the ribs, forcing the core to hold the torso upright without a bench or seat. Keep the hips square and the pull strong through the elbow; the kneeling stance quietly doubles as a hip-flexor stretch."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kneeling-hip-flexor-stretch", "Kneeling Hip Flexor Stretch", "Abs", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The kneeling hip flexor stretch is a stretch targeting greater flexibility in the front side of the hips. It targets a chronically tight muscle group and is often performed as part of a dynamic warm-up, a cool-down, or as recovery work after training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-kneeling-hip-thrust", "Kneeling Hip Thrust (Band)", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The banded kneeling hip thrust kneels tall with a band over the pelvis and pushes the hips forward to full extension, training the glutes in the standing hip-extension pattern. Keep the ribs down and squeeze the glutes to finish; the band's pull rises toward lockout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kneeling-wrist-stretch", "Kneeling Wrist Stretch", "Forearms", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The kneeling wrist stretch kneels and places the backs of the hands on the floor with fingers toward the knees, leaning gently to extend the wrists. It is a favorite of lifters and climbers; ease off if any position produces sharp pain."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("l-sit", "L Sit", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The L sit holds the straight legs out in front of the body while supported on the hands, on the floor or on parallel bars. It builds tremendous hip-flexor and triceps strength and core control; start with one leg tucked or brief 5-second holds."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("landmine-press", "Landmine Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The landmine press presses the end of a barbell set in a landmine attachment or corner up and forward from the shoulder. The angled path spares the shoulder joint while still loading the delts and chest; step into it and drive through the front foot."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("lat-pulldown", "Lat Pulldown", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The lat pull-down is a cable-based exercise that's ubiquitous in gyms around the world. This back builder is easy to learn and highly effective at building back size and strength. It's usually trained in moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set. If grip strength is a limitation, you can wear wrist straps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ball-leg-curl", "Leg Curl (Ball)", "Hamstrings", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The exercise ball leg curl is an exercise targeting the hamstrings. In particular, it targets the knee flexion function of the hamstrings, as opposed to deadlift variations, which target the other hamstring function, hip flexion. Many lifters opt for this exercise when they don't have access to a lying leg curl machine, but it is worth considering even if you already do machine curls."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("leg-extension", "Leg Extension", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The leg extension is an isolation exercise for the quads."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("leg-press", "Leg Press", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The leg press is a machine exercise targeting the quadriceps. It’s favorite among lifters who prefer the seated position to standing with a load on the spine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("legs-up-the-wall", "Legs-Up-the-Wall Pose", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Legs-up-the-wall pose rests the legs vertically against a wall while the back lies on the floor. It drains fatigue from the legs, relaxes the hamstrings and lower back, and is a classic recovery posture after hard training days."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("lizard-stretch", "Lizard Stretch", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The lizard stretch drops into a deep low lunge with both hands inside the front foot, sinking the hips toward the floor. It opens the hip flexors and adductors at once; forearms down or the back knee lifted both work, whichever keeps the front knee tracking out."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("locust-pose", "Locust Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Locust pose lies face-down and lifts the chest, arms and legs against gravity, strengthening the entire back side of the body. Keep the lift modest and the neck neutral; it is one of the safest ways to build spinal-erector endurance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("low-lunge", "Low Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The low lunge rests the back knee on the floor in a long split stance, opening the hip flexors of the trailing leg. Keep the torso tall and tuck the pelvis before deepening; adding a gentle side bend or reach multiplies the stretch."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("low-lunge-to-half-split", "Low Lunge to Half Split", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "This flow rocks from a low lunge into a half split and back, alternating a hip-flexor stretch with a hamstring stretch in one continuous movement. Move slowly with the breath; it is a highly efficient warm-up for running, squatting and kicking patterns."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("lunge", "Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The lunge steps forward or backward into a split stance and sinks until both knees approach right angles, training the legs one side at a time. Keep the front knee over the shoe and the torso tall; alternate legs or complete one side first, both are fine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("leg-curl", "Lying Leg Curl", "Hamstrings", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The lying leg curl is a popular machine-based exercise for the legs, particularly the hamstrings. It is similar to the seated leg curl, but the open hip angle in the lying leg curl may incorporate more glute and calf activation. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a leg pre-exhaust or as a muscle-building movement for lower-body training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("lying-leg-raise", "Lying Leg Raise", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The alternating lying leg lift is a popular bodyweight exercise targeting the muscles of the abs, the rectus abdominis or “six-pack” muscles in particular. It can be performed for time or reps in the core-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("lying-tricep-extension", "Lying Tricep Extension", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The lying cable triceps extension is an isolation exercise performed lying on either a bench or the floor. This movement is usually performed for moderate to high reps for a burn and pump as part of an arm-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-lying-tricep-extension", "Lying Triceps Extension (EZ-Bar)", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar lying triceps extension lowers the angled bar to the forehead or slightly behind from a locked-out press, isolating the triceps under a deep stretch. Keep the elbows fixed in space pointing at the ceiling; only the forearms travel."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("assisted-dips", "Machine Assisted Dips", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The machine assisted dip kneels or stands on the counterweight platform of an assisted dip station and performs dips with part of the bodyweight removed. Set the assist as low as honest reps allow; the movement pattern and elbow position stay identical to the free-weight dip, which makes it the standard bridge toward full dips."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-back-extension", "Machine Back Extension", "Spinal Erectors", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine back extension trains the spinal erectors through a controlled hinge and raise against a weight stack. Hinge from the hips, keep the ribs down and squeeze the glutes to finish; the movement should come from the hips, not a whip of the lower back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-bicep-curl", "Machine Bicep Curl", "Biceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine bicep curl locks the elbows into a pad and moves the handle through a fixed arc, isolating the biceps. The pad removes all swing; let the arm fully straighten at the bottom and squeeze at the top of each rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-calf-raise", "Machine Calf Raise", "Calves", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine seated calf raise is an exercise targeting the calf muscles of the lower leg, particularly the soleus muscle. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set, and occasionally for very high burnout-focused sets of 50-100 total reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-chest-fly", "Machine Chest Fly", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine chest fly is a machine-based exercise targeting the chest. It approximates the motion of a dumbbell fly or cable fly, but is usually performed facing forward and seated upright. The motion is often compared to bear-hugging a tree trunk. The chest fly is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a pre-exhaust or muscle-building upper-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("chest-press-machine", "Machine Chest Press", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The machine chest press is a machine-based exercise targeting the chest. It approximates the motion of a bench press but is usually performed facing forward and seated upright. Many machines offer multiple grip options, such as overhand, neutral (palms facing), or underhand. The chest press is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a pre-exhaust or muscle-building upper-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("hip-abduction", "Machine Hip Abduction", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine hip abduction pushes the legs apart against a weighted pad, isolating the glute medius and upper glutes. Sit tall with the chest up and control the return; a slight forward lean changes the feel but should be deliberate, not a slump."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-preacher-curl", "Machine Preacher Curl", "Biceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine preacher curl is a popular exercise to build size and strength in the biceps. It approximates the motion of a free-weight preacher curl, but uses a selectorized weight stack or plate-loaded machine. Many such machines offer different grips and grip widths. Machine biceps curls are often done for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of the upper-body or arm-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-seated-crunch", "Machine Seated Crunch", "Abs", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine seated crunch loads a crunch through a padded lever, letting the abdominals train against a measurable stack. Curl the ribs toward the pelvis and exhale hard at the top; the weight should move because the trunk rounds, not because the arms pull."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-shoulder-press", "Machine Shoulder Press", "Front Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The machine shoulder press is a machine-based exercise targeting the deltoid muscles. It approximates the motion of a free-weight shoulder press, but uses a selectorized weight-stack or plate-loaded machine. Many such machines also allow you to choose between different grips and grip widths. Machine shoulder presses are often done for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of the upper-body or shoulder-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("machine-triceps-extension", "Machine Triceps Extension", "Triceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine triceps extension is an isolation exercise targeting the tricep muscles."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("medicine-ball-slam", "Medicine Ball Slam", "Abs", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The medicine ball slam is an explosive exercise for developing power, strength, and speed. Muscles worked include the abdominals, legs, triceps, shoulders, and lats. The slams are usually done in rapid succession to complete one set. It can also be a component of a power or athleticism-focused circuit workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("mountain-climbers", "Mountain Climbers", "Abs", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The mountain climber is a popular bodyweight exercise targeting the muscles of the core, as well as the shoulders, hips, and cardiovascular system. It involves lifting one knee to the chest at a time from a straight-arm plank position. It can be performed for time or reps as part of a dynamic warm-up, for bodyweight cardio or conditioning, or as no-equipment dynamic core training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("mountain-pose", "Mountain Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Mountain pose is standing tall with the weight balanced evenly across both feet and the crown of the head reaching up. It looks like nothing, but it trains the posture, breath and alignment that every other exercise inherits; scan the body from the ground up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("muscle-snatch", "Muscle Snatch", "Lateral Deltoids", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The muscle snatch pulls the bar overhead without any rebend of the knees or elbows, keeping the arms straight through the finish. It is a strength and position drill for the snatch; go lighter than a power snatch and punch the bar up, then lock it hard."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("muscle-ups", "Muscle Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The muscle up pulls from a hang all the way to support above the bar or rings, blending a high pull with a transition and a dip. It demands pulling power, a false grip or a strong kip and patient technique work; progress through high pulls and chest-to-bar reps first."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("neck-side-stretch", "Neck Side Stretch", "Upper Traps", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The neck side stretch tilts the ear toward the shoulder, optionally assisting with a light hand. Keep the opposite shoulder heavy and relaxed; 20 to 30 seconds per side releases the upper trapezius after desk time or heavy carries."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("negative-pull-ups", "Negative Pull Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The negative pull-up is a multijoint bodyweight exercise that focuses on just one half of the traditional pull-up: the lowering, eccentric, or \"negative\" portion. Because muscles are stronger eccentrically than they are concentrically, many people can perform negatives even if they can't perform full pull-ups. For this reason, it is a popular training tool to help people earn their first pull-up rep, or as a back-off exercise after doing full pull-ups."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("neutral-grip-pull-ups", "Neutral Grip Pull Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The neutral-grip pull-up is a multijoint bodyweight exercise in which the reps are performed with the palms facing one another. Like other pull-up variations, it builds strength and muscle in the upper back, biceps, and core. It can be used as a more shoulder-friendly alternative to straight-bar pull-ups, or to help perform more reps than you can perform overhand."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("nordic-hamstring-curl", "Nordic Hamstring Curl", "Hamstrings", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The Nordic hamstring curl anchors the ankles and lowers the body forward from the knees with straight hips, fighting the fall with the hamstrings alone. It is one of the most effective hamstring-strength and injury-prevention drills in sport; control the descent and push back up only as far as strength allows."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-offset-reverse-lunge-and-press", "Offset Reverse Lunge and Press (Kettlebell)", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell offset reverse lunge and press holds the bell on one side while stepping back and pressing it up, forcing the core to fight the off-center load through the whole rep. Keep the torso square to the front; the offset is the point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-bicep-curl", "One Arm Kettlebell Bicep Curl", "Biceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The one-arm kettlebell bicep curl curls a single bell with the free hand free, letting the trunk stay honest and adding an anti-rotation demand. Keep the elbow fixed at the side; the bell's offset handle keeps the wrist neutral."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-floor-glute-bridge-press", "One Arm Kettlebell Floor Glute Bridge Press", "Glutes", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell floor glute bridge press bridges the hips up and presses a bell overhead from the floor at the same time. The bridge locks the pelvis while the shoulder works independently; it builds glute strength and overhead stability in one drill."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-floor-press", "One Arm Kettlebell Floor Press", "Pectorals", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell floor press presses a bell from the floor with the shoulder blade pinned, limiting the range safely and hammering the triceps and chest. The floor spots every rep, so heavy singles are fair game; keep the working elbow at about 45 degrees."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-front-squat", "One Arm Kettlebell Front Squat", "Quadriceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell front squat squats with a single bell racked, demanding enormous anti-rotation strength from the trunk. The obliques on the unloaded side work as hard as the legs; keep the ribs from flaring toward the bell."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-push-press", "One Arm Kettlebell Push Press", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell push press dips the legs slightly and punches a racked bell overhead, borrowing leg drive to move heavier loads than the strict press. Time the dip and drive together; the arm finishes what the hips start."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-row", "One Arm Kettlebell Row", "Lats", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell row rows a single bell to the hip from a hinged stance, loading the lat while the trunk resists rotation. Keep the shoulders square to the floor and pull the elbow past the ribs; the free hand can brace on the thigh."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-shoulder-press", "One Arm Kettlebell Shoulder Press", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell shoulder press presses a racked bell strictly overhead, training each shoulder and the anti-side-bend core separately. Keep the forearm vertical under the bell and the ribcage stacked over the pelvis; equal sets per side."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-swing", "One Arm Kettlebell Swing", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell swing swings a single bell between the legs and to chest height, adding anti-rotation work for the trunk and a grip challenge for the hand. Hike the bell back like a football snap and let the hip snap do the throwing; switch hands at the top or between sets."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-dumbbell-push-press", "One-Arm Dumbbell Push Press", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm dumbbell push press drives a single bell overhead with a leg dip and punch, letting one shoulder handle heavier loads while the core resists the sideways lean. Keep the torso square to the front; the dip is small and the drive is violent."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-dumbbell-swing", "One-Arm Dumbbell Swing", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm dumbbell swing swings a single bell between the legs to shoulder height with a powerful hip snap, the dumbbell cousin of the kettlebell swing. Keep the arm long and let the hips do the throwing; hike the bell back before every rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-bottoms-up-press", "One-Arm Kettlebell Bottoms-Up Press", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm kettlebell bottoms-up press presses a bell held upside down by its handle, demanding relentless wrist and shoulder stability. Go far lighter than a normal press; the bell should wobble no more than a few degrees on the way up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-kettlebell-tricep-kickback", "One-Arm Kettlebell Tricep Kickback", "Triceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The one-arm kettlebell tricep kickback hinges forward and extends a bent elbow back to lockout, isolating the triceps. Keep the upper arm parallel to the floor and still; only the forearm moves, and the bell's balance demands a steady wrist."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-landmine-press", "One-Arm Landmine Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm landmine press presses the angled bar with a single arm from the shoulder, training each side while the core resists the twist. The landmine's path lets the shoulder blade move freely; stand staggered and press up and forward along the bar's line."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-lat-pulldown", "One-Arm Lat Pulldown", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm lat pulldown pulls a single handle down beside the body, letting each lat work through its own range without the stronger side covering. Keep the torso square and reach tall at the top; the stretch under load is the best part of the rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-single-leg-dumbbell-romanian-deadlift", "One-Arm Single-Leg Dumbbell Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-leg dumbbell Romanian deadlift is a movement targeting the muscles of the hamstrings, glutes, and back. The difference between a Romanian deadlift and a stiff-legged deadlift is the amount of knee bend. The knees are only slightly bent in a stiff-legged deadlift, while they bend more in a Romanian deadlift. Single-leg deadlifts are generally performed for low to moderate reps, such as 8-10 reps per set, with a focus on maximizing the hamstring stretch rather than moving heavy weight. If balance is a challenge, this movement is still very effective if performed while holding on to a stable object for support."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("one-arm-single-leg-kettlebell-romanian-deadlift", "One-Arm Single-Leg Kettlebell Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The one-arm single-leg kettlebell Romanian deadlift hinges on one leg while holding the bell in the opposite hand, stacking rotation onto balance. Keep the hips square to the floor, since the temptation to open up is the drill; move slowly and touch nothing."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("overhead-squat", "Overhead Squat", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The overhead squat squats with a bar locked out overhead, demanding mobility, stability and strength from ankles to shoulders at once. It is the deepest test of squat mechanics in the gym; keep the bar over the mid-foot throughout and treat light weights as technique work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("overhead-tricep-extension", "Overhead Tricep Extension", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The band overhead triceps extension is a single-joint exercise focused on the triceps that uses the dynamic resistance of the band. The overhead position of the arms targets the long head of the triceps. Many lifters save this move for last in their triceps workout, performing it for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps or more per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-overhead-extension", "Overhead Tricep Extension (EZ-Bar)", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar overhead tricep extension lowers the angled bar behind the head from an overhead lockout, stretching the long head of the triceps fully. Keep the elbows narrow and pointing forward, and the ribcage down; the stretch is the working part of the rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-overhead-tricep-extension", "Overhead Tricep Extension (Kettlebell)", "Triceps", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell overhead tricep extension lowers a bell behind the head from a locked-out overhead grip and presses it back up. Keep the elbows pointing forward and the ribs down; the stretch at the bottom is the growth position."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("overhead-triceps-stretch", "Overhead Triceps Stretch", "Triceps", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The overhead triceps stretch reaches one arm overhead and bends the elbow so the hand drops between the shoulder blades, then assists with the opposite hand. Keep the ribs down, since arching the back fakes range; it is the go-to reset after pressing or locking out overhead."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pause-deadlift", "Pause Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The pause deadlift stops the pull below the knee for a full breath before finishing, killing momentum and exposing weak positions. Keep the lats engaged and the hips high through the pause; the restart must come from the legs, not a yank."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pause-pull-up", "Pause Pull-Up", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The pause pull up holds the chin over the bar, or the chest at it, for two to three seconds before lowering, building strength through the top range where most pull ups stall. Keep the shoulders down and the body quiet through the hold; no half-rep at the end."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pause-squat", "Pause Squat", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The pause squat sits in the hole for two to three seconds before driving up, removing the stretch reflex and forcing pure strength out of the bottom. Stay tight and keep the brace during the pause; the first inch of the ascent is where the rep is won."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("paused-bench-press", "Paused Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The paused bench press holds the bar dead on the chest for a beat before pressing, killing the bounce and building strength from a dead stop. Keep the shoulder blades pinched and the torso tight through the pause; touch, hold, then drive."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("paused-incline-bench-press", "Paused Incline Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The paused incline bench press holds the bar dead on the upper chest from an incline bench before pressing, removing momentum from the hardest pressing angle. Keep the wrists stacked and the pause quiet, with no sinking into the rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("paused-ohp", "Paused Overhead Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The paused overhead press holds the bar at chin or forehead height before pressing to lockout, eliminating the leg-drive bounce and building strength through the sticking point. Keep the torso braced and the bar path straight through the pause."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pec-deck", "Pec Deck", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The pec deck is a machine-based exercise targeting the chest. It approximates the motion of a dumbbell fly or cable fly, but is usually performed facing forward and seated upright. The motion is often compared to bear-hugging a tree trunk. The chest fly is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a pre-exhaust or muscle-building upper-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pendlay-row", "Pendlay Row", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Pendlay row, named after the well-known strength coach Glenn Pendlay, is a bent-over row variation where each rep starts with the barbell resting on the ground. This eliminates the stretch reflex and forces the lifter to pull with power and a bent-over spine. The Pendlay row can be performed heavy for low reps, but is usually performed for moderate reps, such as 5-8 per set."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pigeon-stretch", "Pigeon Stretch", "Glutes", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The pigeon stretch is a lower-body stretch targeting the glutes, hips, and piriformis. It is similar to the pigeon pose from yoga, but is also popular as part of dynamic warm-ups or cool-downs in strength training. It can also be performed anytime to help combat training-related soreness."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pike-push-ups", "Pike Push Ups", "Front Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The pike push up lowers the head toward the floor from a downward-dog position, pressing the body back up with the shoulders overhead. It is the bodyweight bridge toward handstand push ups; keep the hips high and the elbows tracking about 45 degrees back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-kneeling-side-kick", "Pilates Kneeling Side Kick", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The Pilates kneeling side kick kneels tall and sweeps the top leg forward and back in a controlled arc, training the hip stabilizers and the obliques that keep the torso upright. The trunk does not sway; the leg moves and the body stays. Reach long in both directions."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-leg-pull-back", "Pilates Leg Pull Back", "Spinal Erectors", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The Pilates leg pull back holds a reverse plank position and lifts one leg at a time, challenging the posterior chain and the shoulder stabilizers together. Keep the hips level as the leg lifts; the trunk is a plank and the leg is punctuation."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-leg-pull-front", "Pilates Leg Pull Front", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The Pilates leg pull front holds a high plank and lifts one straight leg slightly off the floor, adding a hip-extension challenge to the anti-rotation plank. Keep the hips square and the shoulders steady; the leg lift is small by design."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-roll-down", "Pilates Roll Down", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The Pilates roll down peels the spine off the floor one vertebra at a time and returns the same way, articulating the entire column. Move slowly enough that each segment gets its moment; it teaches segmental control that carries into every hinge and squat."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-roll-over", "Pilates Roll Over", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The Pilates roll over lies on the back and rolls the legs overhead until the toes reach beyond the head, articulating the spine one vertebra at a time. Keep the legs straight and the movement slow; it demands control more than flexibility and deserves a warmed-up spine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-saw", "Pilates Saw", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The Pilates saw sits with the legs wide and rotates the torso over one leg, reaching the opposite hand past the little toe. Keep the sitting bones planted and rotate from the waist rather than collapsing; it combines spinal rotation with a hamstring stretch."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-side-bend", "Pilates Side Bend", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The Pilates side bend holds a side plank on one hand and arcs the top arm overhead, lifting the hips into a long side curve. It trains the obliques and shoulder stabilizers through full range; keep the body in one plane with no twisting toward the floor."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-spine-stretch-forward", "Pilates Spine Stretch Forward", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The Pilates spine stretch forward sits tall with the legs extended and curls the spine forward over the thighs one segment at a time. Reach long rather than round hard; it lengthens the back line of the body while reinforcing deep-core engagement."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pilates-spine-twist", "Pilates Spine Twist", "Obliques", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The Pilates spine twist rotates the upright torso from side to side on a tall, still lower body. The pelvis stays perfectly level and only the ribs and head turn; it sharpens rotational control that transfers to throws, swings and punches."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pistol-squat", "Pistol Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The pistol squat descends on a single leg with the other extended straight ahead, the most demanding bodyweight squat there is. Keep the heel down, the shin forward and the hips square; box, heel-elevated and counterweight versions bridge the gap."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("planche", "Planche", "Pectorals", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The planche supports the entire body horizontally on the hands with straight arms and no contact but the palms. It is a years-long skill progression loading the shoulders, wrists and core beyond almost any other bodyweight hold; lean progressively and train tucked versions first."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plank", "Plank", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The elbow plank is a popular isometric abdominal exercise. It is common in all types of exercise programs, as well as in group fitness and yoga classes. It targets the muscles of the core, the deep core or transversus abdominis in particular. It is also often prescribed for time to help back pain or to teach proper bracing."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plate-pinch", "Plate Pinch", "Forearms", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "isolation", "The plate pinch holds one or more weight plates between a straight thumb and fingers at the side. It is a pure grip-strength tool that hits the thumb side of the hand that most other grip work misses; 20 to 45 second holds with both hands work well."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plate-pullover", "Plate Pullover", "Lats", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The plate pullover lies on a bench and arcs a weight plate from over the chest to behind the head and back, keeping the arms nearly straight. It stretches the lats and chest under load while training the core to keep the ribs down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plate-loaded-donkey-calf-raise", "Plate-Loaded Donkey Calf Raise", "Calves", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The plate-loaded donkey calf raise bends the torso forward and drives the heels up against a pad on the lower back, putting the calves under a long, heavy stretch. Let the heels sink deep at the bottom and pause at the top; the donkey angle places tension on the soleus and gastrocnemius alike."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plate-loaded-glute-drive", "Plate-Loaded Glute Drive", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The plate-loaded glute drive is a hip thrust machine: the back pads against a bench-style support while the feet drive the platform up through hip extension. The machine handles setup and stability so the glutes can be loaded progressively and heavily."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plate-loaded-lateral-raise", "Plate-Loaded Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The plate-loaded lateral raise isolates the side delts through a machine's fixed arc with lever arms or a loaded plate. The track removes momentum from the equation; raise to shoulder height and control the descent rather than swinging."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plate-loaded-shrug", "Plate-Loaded Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The plate-loaded shrug traps the shoulders against a machine's padded levers so the traps lift a heavy load straight up. Shrug up and back, pause briefly at the top, and let the arms stay long; the traps, not the biceps, move the weight."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plow-pose", "Plow Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Plow pose rolls the legs overhead until the toes touch the floor behind the head, stretching the entire back line of the body. Support the back with the hands and keep the weight off the neck; roll out slowly and avoid it with any cervical discomfort."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plyo-lunge", "Plyo Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Other", "plyometric_explosive", "compound", "The plyo lunge jumps to switch the legs mid-air and lands back in a lunge, repeating explosively. Land soft with the hips back and the front knee tracking over the shoe; it builds lower-body power and raises the heart rate fast."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("plyo-push-up", "Plyo Push-Up", "Pectorals", "Other", "plyometric_explosive", "compound", "The plyo push-up is a bodyweight exercise aimed at increasing upper-body power and strength. It targets the muscles of the chest, triceps, and shoulders, and can be trained in low reps for power and strength, or higher reps for conditioning."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("preacher-curl", "Preacher Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar preacher curl is a classic exercise for building bigger biceps. This move is all about isolation. With the back of the upper arms against the pad, it’s next to impossible to cheat. Go light, get a great pump, and build that peak!"),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("preacher-hammer-curl", "Preacher Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The preacher hammer curl curls a bell with a neutral grip over a preacher pad, which fixes the elbow and puts the brachialis and forearm under strict tension. Let the arm straighten fully at the bottom of the pad's angle; the stretch there is the point of the exercise."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pseudo-planche-push-ups", "Pseudo Planche Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The pseudo planche push up shifts the hands down beside the hips and leans the shoulders well past the wrists, loading the front delts and straight-arm strength toward the planche. Keep the body hollow and the lean honest; the more forward the shoulders, the harder the rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pull-up", "Pull-Up", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The pull-up is a multijoint bodyweight exercise that builds strength and muscle in the upper back, biceps, and core. It is often used as a measurement tool in military or tactical fitness tests, and is an excellent gauge of \"relative strength\" which is strength in relation to body weight."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("puppy-pose", "Puppy Pose", "Lats", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Puppy pose kneels with the hips over the knees and walks the hands forward, melting the chest toward the floor. It opens the lats, chest and thoracic spine while sparing the lower back; rest the forehead on the ground and breathe into the upper back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("push-jerk", "Push Jerk", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The push-jerk is a movement in which the barbell is pushed from the front rack position to overhead with help from the legs. It differs from the push-press in that the lifter dips under the bar rather than simply pushing it up. It is popular in CrossFit workouts and Olympic weightlifting, as well as power training for athletics. It is usually performed for low reps, focusing on power and movement quality."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("push-press", "Push Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The push-press is an overhead press variation in which the barbell is pushed upward with assistance from the lower body. This \"push\" helps the weight travel through the sticking point of a strict press. The push-press is commonly performed as part of barbell complexes, power training for all types of athletics, or as a powerful shoulder exercise."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("push-up", "Push-Up", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The push-up is a popular bodyweight exercise that is commonly used in military and tactical physical fitness tests. It’s a classic movement to build upper-body muscle and strength, emphasizing the chest, triceps, and shoulders, but also working the upper back and core."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stability-ball-push-up-hands-on-ball", "Push-Up (Hands on Ball) (Ball)", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The stability ball push-up with hands on the ball sets the palms on the unstable ball itself, demanding enormous shoulder stability through the press. Keep the elbows tracking back and the ball from sliding; go narrow in range before chasing depth."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("pyramid-pose", "Pyramid Pose", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Pyramid pose stands in a short forward split with both legs straight and folds the torso over the front leg. It delivers a clean hamstring and calf stretch; square the hips to the front foot and hinge at the hip crease, not the waist."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("rack-pull", "Rack Pull", "Spinal Erectors", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The rack pull is a variation of the deadlift performed from an elevated height, either using boxes, safety pins, or the safeties in a squat rack. It targets all the same muscles as the deadlift, including the glutes, hamstrings, upper back, lower back, and grip. It can be used to strengthen deadlift lockout, but also as a deadlift alternative for people who can't deadlift from the floor without pain."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("rear-delt-fly", "Rear Delt Fly", "Rear Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The seated rear delt fly is an upper-body exercise that targets the real deltoid of the shoulder. By performing the movement seated, it eliminates momentum from the rest of the body. This movement is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of the upper-body or shoulder-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("barbell-rear-delt-row", "Rear Delt Row (Barbell)", "Rear Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell rear delt row rows a barbell high toward the chest with the elbows flared wide, biasing the rear delts and upper back over the lats. Hinge to near-parallel and keep the neck long; think of pulling with the elbows, not the hands."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-rear-delt-stretch", "Rear Delt Stretch (Band)", "Rear Deltoids", "Bands", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The banded rear delt stretch crosses a band in front of the body and draws the arms across the chest, opening the back of the shoulders. Keep the neck relaxed and the shoulders down; a light, sustained pull does more than a hard yank."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-crunches", "Reverse Crunches", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The reverse crunch is a popular exercise targeting the abdominals, particularly the lower half. It’s easy to perform on either the floor or a flat bench. Many lifters think of this as a companion to the crunch, which targets the upper abdominals more than the lower."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-curl", "Reverse Curl", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The reverse curl curls a barbell with the palms facing down, shifting the work from the biceps to the brachialis and forearm extensors. Keep the elbows pinned and the wrists straight, since curling with bent wrists cheats the forearms out of the rep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-grip-bent-over-row", "Reverse Grip Bent Over Row", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The reverse-grip bent-over row is a compound exercise used to build strength and size in both the lower and upper back. It targets nearly all of the muscles in the back, but particularly the lats, rhomboids, and lower back. It is thought to target the biceps and lats a bit more directly than overhand barbell rows. Because it can be loaded heavily, it is a popular movement in strength and muscle-focused upper-body workouts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-reverse-grip-bent-over-row", "Reverse Grip Bent Over Row (Smith Machine)", "Lats", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine reverse-grip bent-over row rows the guided bar with palms up, shifting emphasis toward the lats and lower biceps. The fixed path takes balance out of the hinge; keep the torso close to parallel and pull the bar to the lower ribs."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-grip-lat-pulldown", "Reverse Grip Lat Pulldown", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The reverse-grip lat pull-down is variation on the lat pull-down that targets the lower lats more than an overhand grip. Because the hands are relatively close together, it also increases the range of motion beyond wider-grip pull-down variations. It can be performed for low reps, such as 5-6 per set, for back strength, or for higher reps for size."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-reverse-grip-row", "Reverse Grip Row (EZ-Bar)", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The EZ-bar reverse-grip row rows the bar to the lower ribs with palms up, shifting the pull toward the lats and biceps. Keep the torso hinged close to parallel and the elbows tracking close to the body; squeeze the shoulder blades at the top."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-lunge", "Reverse Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The reverse lunge is a lower-body exercise targeting the quads, glutes, and hamstrings. The reverse lunge places more emphasis on the glutes than other lunge variations."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-reverse-lunge", "Reverse Lunge (Smith Machine)", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine reverse lunge steps back under a guided bar, keeping the load traveling straight up and down. The fixed path spares the knees and lets balance take a back seat, which suits higher loads and stricter form."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-nordic-curl", "Reverse Nordic Curl", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The reverse Nordic curl kneels tall and hinges the whole body backward from the knees, loading the quads through a deep stretch while the hips stay extended. Keep the hips locked forward and move slowly; it is the quad's answer to the Nordic hamstring curl."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-plank", "Reverse Plank", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The reverse plank faces the ceiling, supported on the hands and heels with the body straight. It strengthens the posterior chain through the glutes, hamstrings and spinal erectors while opening the chest and shoulders; push the hips high and keep the neck relaxed."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-plank-dips", "Reverse Plank Dips", "Triceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The reverse plank dip lowers and raises the hips from a reverse plank by bending and straightening the elbows, training the triceps through an unusual extended-shoulder angle. Keep the hips high and the chest open; the shoulders should feel the stretch, not strain."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-tabletop-hip-pulses", "Reverse Tabletop Hip Pulses", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The reverse tabletop hip pulses lift and lower the hips in small pulses from a reverse tabletop, hammering the glutes while the shoulders hold the bridge. Keep the shins vertical and the chest open; squeeze the glutes at the top of every pulse."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("reverse-tabletop-hold", "Reverse Tabletop Hold", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The reverse tabletop hold bridges on hands and feet with the shins vertical and the torso flat, holding the hips at tabletop height. It trains glute and shoulder endurance and opens the front of the shoulders; keep the wrists under the shoulders and squeeze the glutes to hold."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("db-reverse-wrist-curl", "Reverse Wrist Curl (Dumbbell)", "Forearms", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The dumbbell reverse wrist curl trains the wrist extensors, hinging the backs of the hands up from a braced forearm position. These muscles tire fast and respond to light, high-rep work; keep the forearms glued down and the movement in the wrists only."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-reverse-wrist-curl", "Reverse Wrist Curl (Kettlebell)", "Forearms", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The kettlebell reverse wrist curl trains the wrist extensors, resting the forearms on the thighs and letting the backs of the hands hinge the bell up. Light weight and high control; the extensors respond to volume, not grinding singles."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("revolved-chair-pose", "Revolved Chair Pose", "Obliques", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Revolved chair pose twists a chair squat, pressing the hands together and hooking one elbow outside the opposite knee. The legs hold the squat isometrically while the spine rotates; lengthen on the inhale and deepen the twist on the exhale."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("revolved-crescent-lunge", "Revolved Crescent Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Revolved crescent lunge adds a spinal twist to the high lunge, bringing opposite hands together beside the front foot or in prayer at the chest. It is a balance and rotation drill that opens the thoracic spine while the legs hold the stance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ring-dead-hang", "Ring Dead Hang", "Forearms", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The ring dead hang hangs from gymnastic rings with straight arms, adding instability that ordinary bar hangs lack. The grip and shoulder stabilizers work overtime to control the rings; keep the body quiet and the rings from drifting."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ring-dips", "Ring Dips", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The ring dip is an upper-body strength exercise performed on gymnastic rings. It targets the muscles of the chest, triceps, and shoulders, but is also quite taxing to the core. It is generally considered to be more difficult than dips performed on parallel bars. It is common in gymnastics training and CrossFit workouts, but is also a simple way to make dips more challenging for any strength athlete."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ring-face-pull", "Ring Face Pull", "Rear Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The ring face pull leans back holding the rings and pulls the hands apart toward the ears, training the rear delts and rotator cuff against bodyweight. The rings' instability doubles the stabilizer demand; keep the body straight from head to heel and finish with the knuckles beside the ears."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ring-muscle-up", "Ring Muscle-Up", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The ring muscle up pulls from a hang below the rings to a support position above them, the rings adding instability to every phase. The transition demands a deep pull and a fast turn of the elbows; train high pulls and slow transitions before stringing full reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ring-push-up", "Ring Push-Up", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The ring push up performs a push-up with the hands on unstable rings, forcing the chest, shoulders and core to control the wobble through the whole range. Keep the rings from drifting apart and the body in one line; expect fewer reps than the floor version, and that is the point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ring-row", "Ring Row", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The ring row leans back holding the rings and rows the chest up to the hands, an adjustable bodyweight horizontal pull. Walk the feet forward to make it harder, keep the body rigid like a plank and pull the rings to the ribs; it scales from beginner to brutally hard."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("rings-inverted-row", "Rings Inverted Row", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The rings inverted row hangs beneath the rings with the body straight and rows the chest to the rings, the unstable version of the inverted row. Keep the hips in line with the shoulders and the rings from flaring; the wobble under load is what builds the stabilizers."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("romanian-deadlift", "Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The barbell stiff-legged deadlift targets the hamstrings, glutes, lower and upper back, as well as the core. It is a popular accessory movement for the deadlift, but also a muscle-building hamstring movement."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-rdl", "Romanian Deadlift (Smith Machine)", "Hamstrings", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine Romanian deadlift hinges under a bar locked to a vertical track, stretching the hamstrings under a guided load. The bar's path removes forward drift so the hinge stays honest; push the hips back and stop when the hamstrings run out."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("rope-climb", "Rope Climb", "Lats", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The rope climb is an exercise that is used commonly in CrossFit workouts and as a test of upper-body strength. It was even contested at the Olympics in the early 20th century. It targets a wide range of upper-body musculature, including the hands and forearms, shoulders, biceps, lats (latissimus dorsi), and core."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("rowing-machine", "Rowing Machine", "Lats", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The rowing machine drives legs, then body, then arms to move the handle, and reverses the order on the recovery. Done well it is a full-body conditioning tool that spares the joints; drive with the hips, keep the lower back long and resist racing the flywheel."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("running", "Running", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "Running is steady-state locomotion at whatever pace the legs can hold, building aerobic capacity, calf and quad endurance and bone density. Land under the hips with a quick, light cadence and increase volume gradually; it pairs with strength work rather than replacing it."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("russian-twist", "Russian Twist", "Obliques", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The Russian twist is an exercise targeting the abdominals and the obliques. Beginning from a position that resembles stopping midway through a sit-up, it involves twisting side to side. It can be performed with body weight alone or while holding a weight plate or other object. It is performed by alternating sides with each rep and can be done for time or for a specific number of reps as part of the ab-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("scapular-pull-ups", "Scapular Pull Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The scapular pull up hangs from a bar and pulls the shoulder blades down and together without bending the elbows, lifting the body a few inches. It is the foundation drill of all pulling work; keep the arms straight and let the lats do the lifting."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("scissor-kicks", "Scissor Kicks", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The scissor kick lies on the back and alternates small vertical kicks with the straight legs, holding the low abs under constant tension. Keep the lower back pressed into the floor; if it arches, raise the legs higher. Small, fast, controlled kicks."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-barbell-overhead-press", "Seated Barbell Overhead Press", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The seated barbell overhead press presses from an upright bench, removing leg drive so the shoulders do every bit of the work. Keep the lower back against the pad and the bar path just in front of the face; expect the strictest overhead strength test in the rack."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-cable-row", "Seated Cable Row", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The cable seated row is a popular exercise to train the muscles of the upper back, including the lats (latissimus dorsi), traps, rhomboids, and rear deltoids, using a cable stack. It also targets the biceps to a lesser degree. The cable row can work well in a variety of rep-ranges, but is most popular in muscle-building workouts or as an accessory movement for strength workouts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-calf-raise", "Seated Calf Raise", "Calves", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The machine seated calf raise is an exercise targeting the calf muscles of the lower leg, particularly the soleus muscle. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set, and occasionally for very high burnout-focused sets of 50-100 total reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-dumbbell-curl", "Seated Dumbbell Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The seated dumbbell biceps curl is a standard exercise for building bigger biceps. While dumbbell biceps curls can be performed standing, this version involves sitting on a bench."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-dumbbell-lateral-raise", "Seated Dumbbell Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The seated dumbbell lateral raise is an exercise that targets the shoulder muscles, particularly the medial or middle head of the deltoids. Performing it on a bench helps enforce strict form and eliminate swinging or using momentum to move the weights. Lateral raise variations are usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of the upper-body or shoulder-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-db-press", "Seated Dumbbell Shoulder Press", "Front Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The seated dumbbell shoulder press is a popular exercise for building muscle and strength in the shoulders. The triceps also work hard in this lift. Many lifters find using dumbbells to be more comfortable and shoulder-friendly for this press variation than a barbell. This can be the primary pressing movement on an upper-body or shoulder day, or an effective accessory movement later in a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-dumbbell-tricep-extension", "Seated Dumbbell Tricep Extension", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The seated dumbbell tricep extension lowers a bell behind the head from an upright seat, the back support removing any temptation to arch. Keep the elbows narrow and pointing forward and let the bell sink deep behind the head; the triceps work hardest in the bottom half."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-forward-fold", "Seated Forward Fold", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The seated forward fold extends the legs and hinges the chest toward the toes, stretching the hamstrings and lower back. Lead with the sternum and keep the spine long; holding the feet or shins with a flat back beats yanking the head down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-seated-hip-abduction", "Seated Hip Abduction (Band)", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The banded seated hip abduction pushes the knees apart against a band while seated, isolating the upper glutes with nothing but a loop. Sit tall and keep the feet still; the knees travel and the torso does not."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-leg-curl", "Seated Leg Curl", "Hamstrings", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The leg curl is a popular machine-based exercise for the legs, particularly the hamstrings. It comes in seated and lying varieties, but the seated leg curl machine is more common in commercial gyms. Leg curl variations are usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a leg pre-exhaust or as a muscle-building movement for lower-body training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-smith-machine-shoulder-press", "Seated Smith Machine Shoulder Press", "Front Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The seated Smith machine shoulder press presses the guided bar overhead from an upright bench. The vertical track removes stabilization work, so the delts and triceps can be loaded strictly; set the bench so the bar descends just in front of the face."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-spinal-twist", "Seated Spinal Twist", "Obliques", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The seated spinal twist plants one foot outside the opposite knee and rotates the upright torso toward it. Lengthen the spine on the inhale and rotate a little more on each exhale; it mobilizes the thoracic spine and the hips together."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("seated-straddle-stretch", "Seated Straddle Stretch", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The seated straddle stretch opens the legs into a wide V and folds the chest toward the floor. Hinge from the hips with a long spine and walk the hands forward slowly; the adductors respond to patience far better than to force."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-shoulder-press", "Shoulder Press (Smith Machine)", "Front Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine shoulder press is a machine-based exercise targeting the deltoid muscles. The Smith machine mimics a barbell but provides more stability. This movement can be programmed in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle and strength endurance. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a shoulder or upper-body focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-lunge", "Side Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The side lunge steps wide to one side and sinks the hip back over the bent leg while the other leg straightens, training the legs in the frontal plane. Keep the bent knee over the shoe and the chest up; push the floor away to return rather than flopping up."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-lying-hip-adduction", "Side Lying Hip Adduction", "Hip Adductors", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The side-lying hip adduction lifts the bottom leg against the floor from a side-lying position, isolating the inner thigh of the down leg. Keep the top knee bent and planted in front; small, controlled lifts with the toe pointed forward."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-plank", "Side Plank", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The side plank is a popular isometric abdominal exercise focusing on the oblique muscles, as well as the transversus abdominis or deep core muscles. It is sometimes paired with the curl up and bird dog as the “McGill big three” for core strength and stability. It is performed for time and can work in a warm-up or in the core-focused portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-plank-leg-lift-hold", "Side Plank Leg Lift Hold", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The side plank leg lift hold raises the top leg off the bottom one inside a side plank and keeps it there. It stacks a glute-medius challenge on top of the obliques' isometric work; if the hips sag, return to a plain side plank first."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-plank-leg-lift", "Side Plank with Leg Lift", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The side plank with leg lift holds a side plank while raising the top leg, stacking a glute medius challenge onto the obliques' isometric hold. Keep the hips high and the body in one plane; if the hips sag, earn the leg lift back with plain side planks first."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-lying-hip-abduction", "Side-Lying Hip Abduction", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The side-lying hip abduction lifts the top leg from a side-lying position, the classic glute medius builder. Keep the leg in line with the body rather than drifting forward, and the pelvis still; the range is small and the burn is the point."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-lying-hip-abduction-hold", "Side-Lying Hip Abduction Hold", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "isolation", "The side-lying hip abduction hold keeps the top leg lifted from a side-lying position, isolating the hip abductors isometrically. Keep the leg in line with the body rather than drifting forward, and the pelvis still; the burn should sit deep in the side of the hip."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-lying-hip-adduction-hold", "Side-Lying Hip Adduction Hold", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "isolation", "The side-lying hip adduction hold lifts the bottom leg against the floor from a side-lying position and holds it up, targeting the inner thigh. Keep the hips stacked; the bottom adductors work hard for such a small range."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("side-lying-lateral-raise", "Side-Lying Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The side-lying lateral raise raises a bell from a side-lying position, loading the deltoid of the bottom arm through its strictest arc. The floor removes all swing; raise to just past vertical and lower slowly, since the bottom half of the range is where this version earns its keep."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-tricep-pushdown", "Single Arm Tricep Pushdown", "Triceps", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-arm tricep pushdown extends one arm down against a cable, keeping constant tension from the very top of the rep. Keep the upper arm glued to the side and squeeze fully at lockout; the free hand can steady the torso."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-db-svend-press", "Single Dumbbell Svend Press", "Pectorals", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single dumbbell Svend press squeezes the top of a bell between the palms and presses it out at chest height, an isometric pec squeeze layered onto a press. Keep the squeeze constant, since the press range can be short; the chest should tremble before the arms do."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-calf-raise", "Single Leg Calf Raise", "Calves", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The single-leg calf raise drives one heel up at a time, doubling the load on each calf without any equipment. Hold a wall for balance only, keep the knee straight and work through the full stretch at the bottom; the free foot can hook behind the working ankle."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-single-leg-deadlift", "Single Leg Deadlift (Kettlebell)", "Hamstrings", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell single-leg deadlift is a variation on the kettlebell deadlift where one foot stays planted on the ground while the other raises into the air at the bottom of the movement. It's popular in lower-body training for strength and muscle building, but also trains balance, grip, and the core muscles."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-extension", "Single Leg Extension", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single-leg leg extension is a popular machine-based exercise for the legs, particularly the quadriceps. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a leg pre-exhaust or as a muscle-building movement for lower-body training. Performing it one leg a time allows you to focus on the mind-muscle connection of each leg and address muscle imbalances."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-glute-bridge", "Single Leg Glute Bridge", "Glutes", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The single-leg glute bridge is a lower-body exercise that targets the glutes, lower back, and hamstrings. It is performed one side at a time."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-lying-leg-curl", "Single Leg Lying Leg Curl", "Hamstrings", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single-leg lying leg curl is a popular machine-based exercise for the legs, particularly the hamstrings. It is similar to the seated leg curl, but the open hip angle in the lying leg curl may incorporate more glute and calf activation. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a leg pre-exhaust or as a muscle-building movement for lower-body training. Performing it one leg a time allows you to focus on the mind-muscle connection of each leg and address muscle imbalances."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-press", "Single Leg Press", "Glutes", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-leg leg press is an exercise targeting the quadriceps one leg at a time. Many lifters prefer the leg press to squatting for several reasons, including the perception that it’s safer for the spine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-romanian-deadlift", "Single Leg Romanian Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-leg dumbbell Romanian deadlift is a movement targeting the muscles of the hamstrings, glutes, and back. The difference between a Romanian deadlift and a stiff-legged deadlift is the amount of knee bend. The knees are only slightly bent in a stiff-legged deadlift, while they bend more in a Romanian deadlift. Single-leg deadlifts are generally performed for low to moderate reps, such as 8-10 reps per set, with a focus on maximizing the hamstring stretch rather than moving heavy weight. If balance is a challenge, this movement is still very effective if performed while holding on to a stable object for support."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-chest-supported-dumbbell-row", "Single-Arm Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row", "Lats", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-arm chest-supported dumbbell row rows one bell from a chest-down bench position, letting each side of the back work through its own range with zero momentum. Pull the elbow past the ribs and rotate the shoulder blade at the top; the pad keeps the spine honest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-dumbbell-overhead-tricep-extension", "Single-Arm Dumbbell Overhead Tricep Extension", "Triceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single-arm dumbbell overhead tricep extension lowers one bell behind the head, letting the elbow track its own natural path while the triceps work in isolation. Keep the upper arm vertical and close to the ear; support the working arm with the free hand at the elbow."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-db-row", "Single-Arm Dumbbell Row", "Lats", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-arm dumbbell row is a popular exercise for building the latissimus dorsi, or lat, muscles of the back. It is most often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per arm or more, as part of a muscle-building upper-body or back-focused portion of a workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-hammer-curl", "Single-Arm Hammer Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single-arm hammer curl curls one bell with a neutral grip, letting each arm work without the stronger side hiding. Keep the elbow pinned to the ribs and squeeze the bell at the top; the wrist stays locked as if swinging a hammer."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-machine-shoulder-press", "Single-Arm Machine Shoulder Press", "Front Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The single-arm machine shoulder press presses one handle overhead from a fixed seat, training each side on its own. The machine stabilizes the path so the working shoulder can focus on pressing; keep the torso upright and resist the twist toward the loaded side."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-arm-plate-loaded-lateral-raise", "Single-Arm Plate-Loaded Lateral Raise", "Lateral Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The single-arm plate-loaded lateral raise raises one lever at a time through the machine's arc, letting each delt work without the stronger side hiding. Keep the elbow slightly bent and the torso still; lighter than the two-arm version is normal."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("single-leg-glute-bridge-hold", "Single-Leg Glute Bridge Hold", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The single-leg glute bridge is a lower-body exercise that targets the glutes, lower back, and hamstrings. It is performed one side at a time."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("sit-ups", "Sit-Ups", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The sit-up is a popular bodyweight exercise focused on the abdominal muscles. It has also been used as part of military, tactical, and scholastic fitness tests for many years. It can be performed for time or reps, with the feet anchored or free, on flat ground or a bench."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("skull-crusher", "Skull Crusher", "Triceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar skullcrusher is a popular exercise targeting the triceps muscles. The EZ-bar is used more often than a straight bar. As for the name, that’s worst-case scenario. The bar should actually come down behind the head. It is usually performed for moderate to high reps as part of an upper body or arms-focused workout. No need to chase a 1RM on this move!"),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("sled-row", "Sled Row", "Lats", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The sled row anchors a rope or straps to a loaded sled and rows the body toward it, or drags the sled hand over hand. It is a joint-friendly horizontal pull that loads the back without shearing the spine; keep the hips low and drive the elbows past the ribs."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-shrug", "Smith Machine Shrug", "Upper Traps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The Smith machine shrug is an upper-body exercise targeting the traps (trapezius muscles). It is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of shoulder or trap-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-squat", "Smith Machine Squat", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine back squat is a machine-based exercise targeting the muscles of the lower body. The Smith machine mimics a barbell but provides more stability. This movement can be programmed in low reps for strength or higher reps for muscle and strength endurance. It is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of a full-body or lower-body workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("snatch", "Snatch", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The snatch takes the barbell from the floor to overhead in one continuous motion, catching it in a deep overhead squat. It is the most athletic lift in the barbell catalog, demanding speed, mobility and precision in one; the bar must stay close and the punch overhead violent."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("sphinx-pose", "Sphinx Pose", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "Sphinx pose lies on the forearms with the elbows under the shoulders and the chest lifted into a gentle backbend. It restores the lumbar curve after sitting and prepares the spine for deeper extension; press the forearms down and keep the glutes soft."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("spider-curl", "Spider Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The spider curl curls two bells from a chest-down incline position with the arms hanging vertical, isolating the biceps at their shortest position. With the shoulder fixed and the elbows unsupported, nothing can swing; squeeze hard at the top and lower for a three-count."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("ez-bar-spider-curl", "Spider Curl (EZ-Bar)", "Biceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The EZ-bar spider curl is an exercise targeting the biceps and performed face down on either an incline bench or on the flat side of a preacher bench. The EZ-bar makes for a more comfortable wrist position, while the angle helps to isolate the biceps and limit involvement of the shoulder muscles. This curl variation is usually performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of upper-body or arm-focused training."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("split-jerk", "Split Jerk", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "olympic_lift", "compound", "The split jerk drives the bar overhead with a dip and a split of the legs, dropping under the bar to catch it with straight arms. It moves more overhead weight than any press; recover the feet front, back, together, and keep the front shin vertical."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("split-squat", "Split Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The split squat holds a static split stance and sinks the back knee toward the floor, training each leg in place without the step of a lunge. Keep the torso tall and the front heel down; the back knee hovers just above the floor at the bottom."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-split-squat", "Split Squat (Smith Machine)", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine split squat holds a static split stance while the guided bar tracks straight over the midfoot. With balance handled by the machine, the front quad and glute take clean, progressive load."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("spoto-press", "Spoto Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Spoto press is a variation of the popular barbell exercise the bench press that builds strength and muscle in the chest, triceps, and shoulders. It differs from the bench press in that the lifter pauses for a few seconds at the bottom of the movement before pressing the bar back up. This pause removes all momentum and increases the time under tension for the chest and shoulder muscles to help develop upper-body musculature and strength."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stability-ball-push-up", "Stability Ball Push-Up", "Pectorals", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The stability ball push-up sets the feet or shins on a stability ball during a push-up, adding instability that forces the core to lock the body into one plank. Keep the hands planted and the ball quiet; the wobblier the ball, the better the core works."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stair-climber", "Stair Climber", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The stair climber is a popular cardio machine found in most commercial gyms. Along with burning a ton of calories, the stair climber also helps build strength in the lower body, especially the glutes. It can work well at higher speeds for interval-style conditioning training, but also works well at slower paces for steady-state cardio."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-calf-raise", "Standing Calf Raise", "Calves", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The standing calf raise is a popular movement to target the calf muscles of the lower leg, and in particular the gastrocnemius muscles. When unweighted, it is usually performed for high reps or for time."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-calf-stretch", "Standing Calf Stretch", "Calves", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The standing calf stretch braces the hands on a wall and steps one foot back, pressing that heel into the floor. A straight back knee biases the gastrocnemius while a bent knee reaches the soleus; hold each version for 20 to 30 seconds."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-forward-fold", "Standing Forward Fold", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The standing forward fold hinges at the hips and lets the torso hang toward the floor, releasing the hamstrings and back. Bend the knees as much as needed to keep the fold comfortable; gravity does the stretching, so no pulling is required."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-forward-fold-to-half-lift", "Standing Forward Fold to Half Lift", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "This flow moves between a standing forward fold and a half lift with a flat back, teaching the hinge and lengthening the hamstrings in both directions. Flow with the breath; the flat-backed half position is where the hinge pattern is trained."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-standing-hip-abduction", "Standing Hip Abduction (Band)", "Glutes", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The banded standing hip abduction swings the leg out to the side against an ankle band, training the glute medius in a standing posture. Keep the torso upright and square, since leaning away cheats the rep; the range is small and that is fine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-standing-hip-adduction", "Standing Hip Adduction (Band)", "Hip Adductors", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The banded standing hip adduction draws the leg across the body against a band anchored to the side, working the adductors from a standing base. Stand tall on the supporting leg and sweep slowly; the crossing leg does the work and the trunk stays quiet."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-standing-curl", "Standing Leg Curl (Band)", "Hamstrings", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The banded standing leg curl curls the heel toward the glute against an ankle-anchored band from a standing position, isolating the hamstrings of the working leg. Keep the thigh vertical and the hips square; control the return, as the band wants to snap the leg back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-quad-stretch", "Standing Quad Stretch", "Quadriceps", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The standing quad stretch is a simple exercise designed to stretch the quadriceps, the big muscles that cover most of the thighs. This is a static stretch, meaning it’s usually performed by holding the stretched position for a certain length of time."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-side-bend", "Standing Side Bend", "Obliques", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "isolation", "The standing side bend stretch is a stretching exercise targeting the obliques, the muscles that run along the side of the core. It is a common inclusion in dynamic warm-ups for the upper body, but can also be performed to help \"limber up\" for lifting or athletics anytime."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-side-bend-flow", "Standing Side Bend Flow", "Obliques", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The standing side bend flow reaches one arm overhead and leans side to side in a continuous rhythm, opening the obliques and the space between the ribs and hip. Keep both feet grounded and the hips still so the bend happens along the spine."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("standing-split", "Standing Split", "Glutes", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The standing split folds forward and lifts one leg toward the ceiling as high as the hamstring allows. It is an intense hamstring stretch of the standing leg and a hip-flexor stretch of the lifted one; keep the hips level rather than chasing leg height."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stationary-bike", "Stationary Bike", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "The stationary bike is a popular cardio machine found in most commercial gyms. Along with burning a ton of calories, the stationary bike also helps develop the lower body. Stationary bikes come in multiple variations, ranging from ones that resemble riding a bike, to recumbent bikes, to fan bikes that work both the arms and the legs. No matter the variation, a stationary bike can work well at higher speeds for interval-style conditioning training, but also works well at slower paces for steady-state cardio."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("step-ups", "Step Ups", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The step up drives up onto a box or bench from a standing start, training each leg through a full, functional range. Keep the whole foot on the box and push through the heel rather than bouncing off the trailing toe; step down softly under control."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stiff-leg-deadlift", "Stiff Leg Deadlift", "Hamstrings", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The stiff-leg deadlift hinges at the hips with only a soft knee bend, loading the hamstrings through a longer stretch than the conventional pull. Keep the bar close to the legs and the spine long; the range ends where the hamstrings stop, not where the plates touch."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("straight-bar-dips", "Straight Bar Dips", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The straight bar dip dips between two parallel bars or on a single straight bar with a pronated grip, a less familiar hand position that loads the chest and triceps afresh. Keep the shoulders down and the lean forward; the unusual grip makes even strong dippers work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("straight-arm-pulldown", "Straight-Arm Pulldown", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The straight-arm bar pull-down is a variation of the straight-arm lat pull-down performed using a cable stack machine. It can either be performed in an athletic upright stance, or slightly bent-over. It both stretches and contracts the muscles of the lats (latissimus dorsi) as well as providing additional activation to the other upper back muscles and the core. Because the triceps are also isometrically involved in the movement, it is usually performed for higher reps, like 8-15 reps or above."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("straight-bar-cable-front-raise", "Straight-Bar Cable Front Raise", "Front Deltoids", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The straight-bar cable front raise raises a bar from a low pulley to shoulder height in front of the body, loading the front delts under constant tension. Keep the arms nearly straight and the torso still; resist the cable all the way down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("strict-curl", "Strict Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The strict curl curls a barbell with the back flat against a wall, removing every ounce of hip and shoulder drive. Keep the elbows pinned and the head, shoulders and glutes touching the wall; it is the purest test of biceps strength there is."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("suitcase-carry", "Suitcase Carry", "Forearms", "Kettlebell", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The suitcase carry walks with a single heavy bell held at one side like a suitcase, forcing the obliques to keep the spine vertical. Stay tall and do not lean away from the load; it is one of the simplest and best anti-lateral-flexion drills."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("sumo-deadlift", "Sumo Deadlift", "Glutes", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The sumo deadlift is a compound movement in which the feet are set in a wide stance with toes pointed out and grip is placed inside of the legs. It is allowed to be performed instead of conventional deadlifts in most powerlifting competitions. The sumo deadlift targets the hamstrings, glutes, and upper back/traps, but also has a greater emphasis on stretching and strengthening the adductor and hip muscles due to the wide stance."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-sumo-high-pull", "Sumo High Pull (Kettlebell)", "Hip Adductors", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell sumo deadlift high pull is a dynamic kettlebell exercise that focuses on the muscles of the posterior chain, including the hamstrings, glutes, traps, rear delts, and upper back. It can be done in lower reps for power or strength, or higher reps for conditioning and fat loss. It can also be a component of a circuit-style kettlebell workout, either for time or reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("sumo-squat", "Sumo Squat", "Quadriceps", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The sumo squat squats with a very wide stance and the toes turned out, shifting load toward the adductors, glutes and inner quads. Keep the knees tracking over the toes and the torso tall; sink between the hips rather than folding forward."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("superman", "Superman", "Spinal Erectors", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "isolation", "The Superman is a bodyweight exercise that consists of isometric holds in an extended spine position. It targets the muscles of the posterior chain, including the muscles of the glutes, spinal erectors, and upper back. It can be performed as part of a dynamic warm-up, or for muscle activation or mobility anytime."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("supine-spinal-twist", "Supine Spinal Twist", "Obliques", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The supine spinal twist lies on the back and drops the knees to one side while the shoulders stay heavy on the floor. It unwinds the lower back and chest after training; rest the top knee on a block if the floor is too far away."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("supine-windshield-wipers", "Supine Windshield Wipers", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The supine windshield wiper lies on the back with the arms wide and sweeps the straight legs side to side like wipers, rotating through the trunk against gravity. Keep the shoulders pinned to the floor; the legs travel and the chest does not. Bend the knees to make it easier."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("supported-shoulderstand", "Supported Shoulderstand", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The supported shoulderstand lifts the legs and hips overhead with the hands supporting the back, resting the weight on the shoulders. It is a gentle inversion that stretches the neck and upper back; enter and exit slowly and keep the head still."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("svend-press", "Svend Press", "Pectorals", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The Svend press is an exercise where the chest is targeted by performing presses while squeezing two weight plates together. It is usually performed for relatively high reps with light weights as an accessory movement to the bench press or as a burnout movement on chest day."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("t-bar-row", "T-Bar Row", "Lats", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The T-bar row rows a landmine-loaded bar from a hinged stance, pulling the weighted end to the chest with both hands. The anchored end removes balance demands so the lats and mid-back can be loaded hard; keep the hinge still and the chest proud."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("banded-terminal-knee-extension", "Terminal Knee Extension (Band)", "Quadriceps", "Bands", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The banded terminal knee extension straightens the knee against a band pulling it backward, retraining the quad's final lockout after injury or long sitting. The band sits behind the knee and the heel stays down; the movement is small, precise and important."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("thoracic-bridge", "Thoracic Bridge", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The thoracic bridge pushes the hips up while reaching one arm overhead to rotate the upper back through the bridge. It restores rotation to a stiff mid-back and stretches the chest; keep the supporting shoulder stacked over the wrist."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("thread-the-needle", "Thread the Needle", "Rear Deltoids", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Thread the needle slides one arm under the chest from all fours, resting the shoulder and ear on the floor. It rotates the thoracic spine and stretches between the shoulder blades; press the top hand down to deepen gently."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("thread-the-needle-flow", "Thread the Needle Flow", "Obliques", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The thread the needle flow alternates reaching one arm to the ceiling and threading it under the body, opening and closing thoracic rotation in rhythm. Move with the breath; it is a reliable warm-up before any pressing, throwing or rotational work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("three-legged-dog", "Three-Legged Downward Dog", "Front Deltoids", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The three-legged downward dog lifts one leg toward the ceiling from a downward dog, loading the standing hip and opening the lifted one. Keep the hips as square as the hamstring allows and press firmly through both hands."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("thruster", "Thruster", "Front Deltoids", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The thruster chains a front squat into an overhead press in one continuous movement, using the squat's drive to punch the bar up. It is the ultimate full-body conditioning lift; ride the bar down into the next squat smoothly and breathe at the top."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("toes-to-bar", "Toes to Bar", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The hanging toes-to-bar is an advanced abdominal exercise in which the lifter touches their feet to a pull-up bar. There are many ways to perform this movement: strict or swinging, arms completely straight or slightly bent, legs straight or bent, each providing unique benefits and challenges. Initially, it may be a difficult move to do for a single rep, but once you build the requisite core, lat, and shoulder strength, it can be performed for higher reps."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("treadmill-running", "Treadmill Running", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "Treadmill running is a popular cardiovascular exercise performed on a machine that is available in most gyms. Performing this exercise on a treadmill allows you to adjust the speed of your running and, depending on the machine, perform it on an adjustable incline. Many machines also have built-in heart rate monitors and timers to help you track your progress."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("tree-pose", "Tree Pose", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Tree pose balances on one leg with the other foot pressed to the inner thigh and the hands in prayer overhead. Press the foot and thigh into each other and fix the gaze on a still point; wobbling is the drill working."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("triangle-pose", "Triangle Pose", "Hamstrings", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Triangle pose stands in a wide stance and tips the torso over the front leg, stacking the hands vertically toward the floor and ceiling. Lengthen both sides of the waist equally; the stretch belongs to the hamstrings and the side body together."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-bicep-curl", "TRX Bicep Curl", "Biceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The TRX bicep curl leans back holding the straps and curls the body up toward the anchors by flexing the elbows, using bodyweight as the resistance. Keep the body rigid like a plank and the elbows fixed at shoulder height; walk the feet in to add load."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-chest-press", "TRX Chest Press", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The TRX chest press holds the straps and lowers the body toward the hands in a push-up against the suspension trainer. Keep the straps under tension and the body in one line; the further the feet walk back, the harder the press."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-face-pull", "TRX Face Pull", "Rear Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The TRX face pull leans back and pulls the straps apart toward the ears, training the rear delts and rotator cuff with bodyweight. Keep the body straight and the elbows high; the finish has the hands wide and level with the head."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-hamstring-curl", "TRX Hamstring Curl", "Hamstrings", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The TRX hamstring curl lies on the back with the heels in the straps and curls the hips up while pulling the heels under the body, a leg curl and bridge in one. Keep the hips high throughout the rep; the slower the pull-in, the more the hamstrings earn."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-lunge", "TRX Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The TRX lunge rests the rear foot in a strap while lunging forward on the front leg, the suspended rear leg adding range and balance demand. Keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the shoe; the strap pulls the rear hip into a deeper stretch."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-pistol-squat", "TRX Pistol Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The TRX pistol squat uses the straps as a balance aid while descending on one leg, letting the athlete learn the depth before owning the balance. Keep the free leg long and the heel down; lighten the grip gradually until the straps carry nothing."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-plank", "TRX Plank", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The TRX plank sets the feet in the straps and holds a plank with the body in one line. The unstable suspension forces the core to fight rotation and sagging simultaneously; keep the shoulders away from the ears and the straps quiet."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-row", "TRX Row", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The TRX row leans back holding the straps and rows the chest up to the hands, the most adjustable horizontal pull in bodyweight training. Keep the body rigid and the straps quiet; walk the feet forward or raise them to scale the load."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-side-plank", "TRX Side Plank", "Abs", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The TRX side plank holds a side plank with the feet suspended in the straps. The obliques and hip stabilizers work against the straps' drift; keep the body long and the bottom shoulder packed."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-squat", "TRX Squat", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The TRX squat squats while holding the straps, using them for balance and assistance on the way down and up. Keep the grip light, since the legs should do the work the straps merely steady; sit back and down between the heels."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-tricep-extension", "TRX Triceps Extension", "Triceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The TRX triceps extension faces away from the anchors and extends the elbows to press the body up from a flexed-arm position, a bodyweight skull crusher. Keep the body straight and the head passing between the hands; the closer the feet to the anchors, the harder the lockout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("trx-y-fly", "TRX Y-Fly", "Rear Deltoids", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The TRX Y-fly leans back holding the straps and raises the arms overhead into a Y, training the lower traps and rear delts through a long lever. Keep the body straight and the elbows soft; the shoulder blades do the lifting, not the arms."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("kettlebell-turkish-get-ups", "Turkish Get Ups (Kettlebell)", "Front Deltoids", "Kettlebell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The kettlebell Turkish get up travels from lying on the back to standing with a bell locked out overhead, through a sequence of deliberate positions. It is equal parts shoulder stability, hip strength and total-body control; move slowly enough that every step could be paused."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("smith-machine-upright-row", "Upright Row (Smith Machine)", "Lateral Deltoids", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The Smith machine upright row is an upper-body exercise targeting the muscles of the traps and shoulders. This movement is often performed for moderate to high reps, such as 8-12 reps per set or more, as part of an upper-body or shoulder-focused workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("upward-dog", "Upward-Facing Dog", "Spinal Erectors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "Upward-facing dog presses the chest up from the floor with straight arms and the thighs hovering, opening the front of the body. Keep the shoulders down away from the ears and push the tops of the feet into the floor to spare the lower back."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("v-ups", "V Ups", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The V-up lies flat and lifts the straight legs and torso together until the hands reach the feet, folding the body into a V at the top. Keep the legs and arms long and the movement controlled; the lower back should stay light on the floor on the way down."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("v-bar-lat-pulldown", "V-Bar Lat Pulldown", "Lats", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The V-bar lat pulldown uses a close neutral handle to pull the weight down to the chest, emphasizing the lower lats and a strong squeeze. Keep the torso near upright and drive the elbows down toward the hips rather than pulling with the hands."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("v-bar-tricep-pushdown", "V-Bar Tricep Pushdown", "Triceps", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The V-bar tricep pushdown uses the angled V handle to press down against a high pulley, angling the hands naturally outward. The neutral grip spares the wrists and elbows; keep the elbows fixed and finish each rep with a full lockout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("v-sit", "V-Sit", "Abs", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The V-sit balances on the sitting bones with the straight legs and torso forming a V, a static core hold that loads the hip flexors and abdominals together. Keep the chest proud and the spine long; if the shoulders round, bring the legs closer to the floor."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("walking", "Walking", "Quadriceps", "Other", "cardio_conditioning", "compound", "Walking is the lowest-cost conditioning there is: a brisk, steady pace that builds the aerobic base and aids recovery between harder sessions. Keep the pace honest enough to raise the breath; hills or a weighted vest add load without impact."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("walking-lunge", "Walking Lunge", "Quadriceps", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The walking lunge is a lower-body exercise that targets the glutes, quads, and hamstrings while challenging stability and balance. It can be performed for reps, time, or distance in the lower-body portion of any workout."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wall-push-ups", "Wall Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The wall push up presses away from a wall while standing, the gentlest push-up progression there is. Keep the body in one straight line from head to heel and the elbows at about 45 degrees; walk the feet further from the wall to add load."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wall-sit", "Wall Sit", "Quadriceps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "The wall sit is an exercise targeting the quadriceps and core muscle. It involves descending into a squat position while leaning against a wall, then holding that position for a predetermined length of time. It's a classic leg endurance-building exercise for basketball players and gymnasts."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("stability-ball-wall-squat", "Wall Squat (Ball)", "Quadriceps", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The stability ball wall squat rolls a ball between the back and the wall as the hips sink into a squat, guiding the spine through a smooth, supported arc. Keep the feet far enough forward that the knees stay behind the toes; the ball keeps the back tall and honest."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("warrior-one", "Warrior I", "Quadriceps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Warrior I squares the hips forward in a long lunge with the arms reaching overhead. It opens the hip flexors of the back leg while the front leg, core and shoulders hold the frame; keep the back heel heavy or lifted consistently on one side."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("warrior-two", "Warrior II", "Quadriceps", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Warrior II holds a wide bent-knee stance with the arms extended in a T and the gaze over the front hand. It builds endurance through the quads and hips while opening the chest; sink the front thigh toward parallel and keep the torso upright."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("warrior-three", "Warrior III", "Glutes", "Other", "isometric_hold_or_carry", "compound", "Warrior III balances on one leg with the torso and the other leg forming a horizontal T. The standing glute and hamstring carry everything while the core keeps the spine long; reach through the back heel as actively as the fingertips."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("weighted-dips", "Weighted Dips", "Pectorals", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "Weighted dips add a plate or dumbbell to the standard chest dip, pushing the triceps and lower chest into serious overload. Keep the lean forward, the elbows tracking back and the range honest; add load only once bodyweight reps are comfortable."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("weighted-pull-up", "Weighted Pull-Up", "Lats", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The weighted pull-up is a more advanced variation of the pull-up exercise in which the lifter adds extra weight to their body. Options for adding weight include a dip belt, weighted vest, chains, a dumbbell placed between the feet or legs, or looping a kettlebell over the foot. Like other pull-up variations, the weighted pull-up builds strength and muscle in the upper back, biceps, and core."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("weighted-wall-crunch", "Weighted Wall Crunch", "Abs", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The weighted wall crunch lies with the legs up a wall and crunches a plate or dumbbell from the chest toward the feet. The wall fixes the hips so the abs do the whole job; curl the ribs toward the pelvis rather than pulling the head."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wide-grip-pull-ups", "Wide Grip Pull Ups", "Lats", "Bodyweight", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The wide-grip pull up hangs with the hands well outside the shoulders and pulls the chest to the bar, shifting the load toward the upper lats and teres major. Keep the shoulders from shrugging and the range full; a slightly shorter chin-over-bar path is normal at this width."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wide-grip-push-ups", "Wide Grip Push Ups", "Pectorals", "Bodyweight", "strength_bodyweight", "compound", "The wide-grip push up spreads the hands well outside the shoulders, biasing the outer chest while shortening the triceps' role. Keep the body in one plank and the elbows from flaring too far; the wider the hands, the more careful the shoulder position."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wide-grip-seated-cable-row", "Wide Grip Seated Cable Row", "Lats", "Cable", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The wide-grip seated cable row rows a wide bar to the upper abdomen with the elbows flared, shifting emphasis toward the rear delts and mid-back. Sit tall, pull the shoulder blades together at the finish and keep the torso from rocking with the weight."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wide-grip-bench-press", "Wide-Grip Bench Press", "Pectorals", "Barbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The wide-grip bench press is a compound exercise targeting the chest and, to a lesser extent, the triceps. The main difference between this exercise and the standard bench press is that the hands are placed farther apart on the bar. Many lifters find they can handle more weight going wide than with narrower grips, although it's also worth noting that plenty of banged-up lifters have said benching with a wide grip may also have contributed to their shoulder injuries and pain. The wide-grip bench is often used as a chest-building movement in chest or upper-body workouts, or as an accessory movement for the traditional bench press."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wide-legged-forward-fold", "Wide-Legged Forward Fold", "Hip Adductors", "Other", "mobility_stretching", "compound", "The wide-legged forward fold stands with the feet wide and folds the torso down between the legs, stretching the adductors and hamstrings together. Keep the weight in the mid-foot and the spine long; hands to the floor or to the ankles both work."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wide-stance-leg-press", "Wide-Stance Leg Press", "Hip Adductors", "Machine", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The wide-stance leg press spreads the feet high and wide on the platform, opening the hips toward the adductors and glutes. Depth still rules; go as deep as the pelvis allows without lifting off the pad, and drive through the whole foot."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("wrist-roller", "Wrist Roller", "Forearms", "Other", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "compound", "The wrist roller winds a weighted rope up by rolling a handle over and over, burning the forearms through their full range. Roll in both directions and resist on the way down; it is the classic finisher for grip day."),
        ExerciseCatalogEntry("zottman-curl", "Zottman Curl", "Biceps", "Dumbbell", "strength_weighted_or_machine", "isolation", "The Zottman curl curls a bell with the palm up, then rotates at the top and lowers it with the palm down, training the biceps on the way up and the brachialis and extensors on the way down. Keep the rotation smooth at the top and the lowering slow; the eccentric is the point."),
    )

    /** Pool names, sorted — the canonical list the pickers and search use. */
    val NAMES = ENTRIES.map { it.name }.sorted()

    private val byName = ENTRIES.associateBy { it.name.lowercase() }
    private val byId = ENTRIES.associateBy { it.id }

    /** The catalog entry for a pool exercise (case-insensitive), or null for custom ones. */
    fun entryFor(name: String): ExerciseCatalogEntry? = byName[name.trim().lowercase()]

    /** The catalog entry for a v5 id, or null. */
    fun entryForId(id: String): ExerciseCatalogEntry? = byId[id]

    /** The precise primary muscle of a pool exercise, or "" outside the pool. */
    fun muscleFor(name: String): String = entryFor(name)?.muscle ?: ""

    /** The equipment category of a pool exercise, or "" outside the pool. */
    fun equipmentFor(name: String): String = entryFor(name)?.equipment ?: ""

    /** The English method description of a pool exercise, or null outside it. */
    fun descriptionFor(name: String): String? = entryFor(name)?.description?.ifBlank { null }

